   * multi-line lane IS the add-a-port gesture now.
   *
   * `"multi"` opens the rail-wide lane — the same `PortLaneOverlay`/
   * `reconcilePortLane` (`./portLane.ts`) machinery Phase 4 built, letting a
   * pasted multi-line block add/remove/reorder ports in one edit; unchanged,
   * just re-triggered. It falls back to `"single"` behaviour when the lane
   * genuinely cannot open here (`laneAvailability` refuses — collapsed real
   * family, row edge, custom mode) rather than leaving a click that does
   * nothing: a control that silently no-ops on every click is worse than one
   * that does the smaller of the two things it knows how to do (truthful
   * rendering's spirit, PORT-EDGE-PLAN-PHASE4.md §5, applied to a click
   * affordance rather than a disabled button). That fallback is why flipping
   * the default is safe on a row edge: it degrades, it never dead-ends.
   */
  editMode?: "single" | "multi";
  /**
   * P-056. The rail-wide lane, OPENED BY THE HOST rather than by an armed
   * port click. Omit — every existing caller — and the lane is uncontrolled
   * exactly as F-1 built it: `editing` arms, a click opens, Escape closes,
   * and nothing below changes.
   *
   * WHY a host would ever want the other half: F-1's trigger CONSUMES the
   * port click (`PortEdgeSlotBox`'s capture listener calls
   * `stopPropagation`, so the click never reaches the port's own label), and
   * a host can already have spent that click. SystemSketch's block rail has:
   * a click on a port's LABEL opens that port's single-line rename, which is
   * P-037's shipped, committed behaviour, while a click on the DOT is the one
   * gesture its wire kit deliberately leaves free ("a press that goes nowhere
   * is a CLICK, and a click on a dot is nothing",
   * `apps/systemsketch/src/shapes/wire/wireGesture.ts`). Arming the rail
   * would have taken the label's click away to give the lane a trigger it
   * already had a better one for. So the host opens the lane itself and keeps
   * both gestures.
   *
   * The lane's own PRECONDITIONS still hold — a controlled `true` on a row
   * edge, in custom mode, or over a collapsed real family opens nothing
   * (`laneAvailability`), because those are facts about whether a lane can be
   * drawn at all, not about who asked for it.
   */
  laneOpen?: boolean;
  /** Called with `false` when the lane closes itself — Escape, Ctrl+Enter, or
   *  a press on a port card. Only meaningful alongside `laneOpen`. */
  onLaneOpenChange?(next: boolean): void;
  /**
   * Follow-up feedback item 3: the label's syntax parser, e.g. `python()`
   * from `@codemirror/lang-python` (already a dependency — `CodeEditor`
   * itself already exposes this exact prop for its own `PseudoCode` story).
   * Omit for plain text. A story-level toggle, not a PortEdge concern of its
   * own — the edge just forwards whatever `LanguageSupport` it's given
   * straight to each port's own editor (F-1: previously the lane's shared
   * `CodeEditor`; now every port's own, individually, same prop reused
   * rather than invented twice — see `PortEdgeSlotBoxProps.labelLanguage`).
   */
  laneLanguage?: LanguageSupport;
  /**
   * Follow-up feedback items 2/4 (docs/FEEDBACK-2026-09-17c-PLAN.md, Lane
   * E): caps how many slots this edge renders. The REST are cut from the
   * track entirely — not grouped, not collapsed, just gone — and replaced
   * by one "+N more" line after the last visible slot, N the real number
   * of PORTS still hidden (a cut slot's own member count, not 1-per-slot,
   * so a cut through a real group still reports accurately).
   *
   * Deliberately its own seam rather than reusing `renderCollapsed`: that
   * prop is handed ONE collapsed family's members (`groupMode==="collapsed"`
   * folding a caller-named group into a single still-occupied slot);
   * `maxVisible` has no family to hand it — the cut can land anywhere,
   * across however many unrelated slots happen to sit past the cutoff, and
   * those slots stop occupying track space altogether rather than shrinking
   * into one. Read and confirmed before adding this: `renderCollapsed`
   * does not fit the shape of this problem.
   *
   * RULING 7 (2026-09-21): this prop owns the DECISION and the COUNT; it
   * does not own the chip that says so. See `renderOverflow` below.
   */
  maxVisible?: number;
  /**
   * RULING 7 — the "+X more" overflow chrome's seam. Zach: "ideally this
   * just comes from the port edge implementation... but YOu do need to
   * provide the chrome to do this... should be in bbox-editor".
   *
   * So the split is: THIS component decides what overflows (`maxVisible`
   * cuts slots, `splitByMaxVisible` in `./portEdge.layout.ts`), counts the
   * real ports behind the cut, and measures where the marker belongs on the
   * track — three things only the component that owns the track can know.
   * The CHROME that draws it is supplied from outside and lives in
   * `editor/` (`editor/portOverflowChip.tsx`'s `PortOverflowChip`), which
   * `components/` may not import (the DAG: components never imports editor).
   * A render prop is therefore the only seam that respects both halves.
   *
   * WHY there is still a default when this is omitted: a capped edge that
   * drew nothing would silently swallow ports, which
   * `feedback_truthful_property_rendering` rules out. The fallback is
   * deliberately the barest possible truthful marker — one line of muted
   * italic text, no border, no interaction — so it reads as the component
   * refusing to lie, not as chrome competing with `editor/`'s.
   */
  renderOverflow?(overflow: PortEdgeOverflow): ReactNode;
  /**
   * The visual variant for every default port on this edge (src/skin/
   * portVariants.ts). Omit — the normal case — and each port reads the
   * document-wide `<html data-bbox-port-variant>`; set it to pin one edge
   * (a gallery of all five). Ignored by a host `renderItem`.
   */
  variant?: PortVariantId;
  /**
   * The IDE move-line chord, applied to PORTS (`keyboardReorder.ts`): while
   * a port's single-line label editor has focus, the axis-matched arrow
   * pair under this modifier moves that port one slot — ↑/↓ on a column
   * edge, ←/→ on a row — and the editor stays open, focused, caret in
   * place. Omit (every existing caller) and no key is claimed.
   *
   * Zach, 2026-09-21: "Can you please show a prototype of how I could
   * reorder the ports in single line mode by using the ctrl up or down (for
   * a column) or (ctrl left right) for a row."
   *
   * Single-line mode only, by construction: the multi-line lane already
   * reorders by moving lines (its own CodeMirror Alt/Ctrl+↑/↓), and Custom
   * mode positions ports by dragging them along the wall rather than by
   * stepping slots, so the chord is not wired there.
   */
  reorderChord?: ReorderChord;
  /**
   * "Multiple returns" (Daily Note 2026-09-22): the SAME rail-wide lane
   * (`editMode: "multi"`) also authors DIVIDERS — a line that is exactly
   * `---` or `|` inserts one at that position; deleting the line removes it
   * (`./returnDividers.ts`'s `isDividerLine`/`reconcileRailLane`).
   *
   * Both omitted (every caller before this feature) and the lane's text is
   * `formatPortLane`'s own output, unchanged — a "---"/"|" line mints an
   * ordinary port exactly as it always has, so no existing rail can have a
   * real port silently reinterpreted as a divider mark. The feature is
   * therefore keyed off `onDividerAfterChange` being SET, not off
   * `dividerAfter` being non-empty (a controlled `[]` still means "this
   * host understands dividers, there just aren't any right now").
   */
  dividerAfter?: number[];
  onDividerAfterChange?(next: number[]): void;
  /**
   * W1 (docs/WORK-ORDER-2026-09-18.md) — Zach's own generalization of the
   * registry: "you can kind of set any anchor points for the cards you
   * want." An escape hatch for a layout that isn't one of the built-in
   * `Distribution` names — when set (with `mode: "auto"`), it OVERRIDES
   * `distribution` entirely, looking `name` up via `portLayout` instead
   * of mapping `distribution.mode`/`scheme` onto a registry key. A story
   * registers a genuinely non-linear layout (an ellipse, an arc) with
   * `registerPortLayout` from OUTSIDE this file and asks for it this way
   * — proof the registry is a real seam, not merely an internal wrapper
   * around the three built-ins (`PortEdge.AnchorLayouts.stories.tsx`).
   */
  anchorLayout?: { name: string; args?: unknown };
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* PortEdgeDndProvider — the shared-DndContext boundary a multi-edge    */
/* story (LeftAndRight/AroundABox) wraps several <PortEdge>s in. Owns    */
/* the cross-edge onDragOver/onDragEnd the story itself provides — the   */
/* "4-around-a-box arrangement belongs to the story" rule (D6).          */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

const PortEdgeDndScope = createContext(false);

/**
 * W1 (docs/WORK-ORDER-2026-09-18.md): dnd-kit's own `closestCenter`
 * ranks by the DRAGGED element's (translated) rect centre versus each
 * droppable's rect centre — a rect-vs-rect measure that made sense when
 * `useSortable` owned the geometry. Now that every card renders at a
 * point a registered `PortLayoutFn` chose (`AnchorSlotCard`, below), the
 * more direct question is which anchor the POINTER ITSELF is nearest to
 * — so this ranks by the raw pointer position instead of the dragged
 * rect. dnd-kit already measures every registered droppable's live rect
 * (`droppableContainers[].rect.current`), and since a card's droppable
 * and its rendered box are the same element, that measured centre IS
 * the anchor in client space — nothing here has to separately re-derive
 * anchor -> client-space itself.
 */
const nearestAnchor: CollisionDetection = ({ droppableContainers, pointerCoordinates }) => {
  if (!pointerCoordinates) return [];
  const collisions = [];
  for (const container of droppableContainers) {
    const rect = container.rect.current;
    if (!rect) continue;
    const cx = rect.left + rect.width / 2;
    const cy = rect.top + rect.height / 2;
    const distance = Math.hypot(cx - pointerCoordinates.x, cy - pointerCoordinates.y);
    collisions.push({ id: container.id, data: { droppableContainer: container, value: distance } });
  }
  return collisions.sort((a, b) => a.data.value - b.data.value);
};

export function PortEdgeDndProvider({
  onDragOver,
  onDragEnd,
  children,
}: {
  onDragOver?(event: DragOverEvent): void;
  onDragEnd?(event: DragEndEvent): void;
  children: ReactNode;
}) {
  const sensors = useSensors(useSensor(PointerSensor, { activationConstraint: { distance: 4 } }));
  return (
    <DndContext sensors={sensors} collisionDetection={nearestAnchor} onDragOver={onDragOver} onDragEnd={onDragEnd}>
      <PortEdgeDndScope.Provider value={true}>{children}</PortEdgeDndScope.Provider>
    </DndContext>
  );
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* Reordering — slot-level (a dragged group carries every member).      */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

/** Reorders `items` by moving `activeSlotId`'s slot next to
 *  `overSlotId`'s, preserving each item's own label/state (the lab's own
 *  `slots` only carry ids — this restores the metadata after arrayMove). */
export function reorderItemsBySlot(items: PortEdgeItem[], slots: Slot[], activeSlotId: string, overSlotId: string): PortEdgeItem[] {
  const oldIndex = slots.findIndex((s) => s.slotId === activeSlotId);
  const newIndex = slots.findIndex((s) => s.slotId === overSlotId);
  if (oldIndex === -1 || newIndex === -1 || oldIndex === newIndex) return items;
  const reordered = arrayMove(slots, oldIndex, newIndex);
  const byId = new Map(items.map((it) => [it.id, it] as const));
  return reordered.flatMap((s) => s.members.map((m) => byId.get(m.id)).filter((it): it is PortEdgeItem => it != null));
}

/**
 * The keyboard chord's reorder: `itemId`'s slot swaps with its neighbour
 * `delta` slots away — through `reorderItemsBySlot`, the SAME operation the
 * pointer drag's `handleDragEnd` runs, so a keyboard move and a drag onto
 * the neighbour produce identical `items` and a host sees one event shape.
 *
 * Returns `items` itself (identity, not a copy) when nothing moves: at
 * either end — no wrap — past the `visibleCount` cut (a `maxVisible` edge
 * never pushes a port behind its own "+N more"), and for a member of a real
 * group. WHY the group case is a no-op rather than a within-group move: a
 * family's member order is the edge's `groups` TEXT (`resolveAssignment`'s
 * `order`), which this component reads but has no callback to write, and a
 * drag has no within-group move either — the whole family travels as one
 * slot. Inventing a write path for it is a design call, not this chord's.
 */
export function moveItemOneSlot(items: PortEdgeItem[], slots: Slot[], visibleCount: number, itemId: string, delta: -1 | 1): PortEdgeItem[] {
  const index = slots.findIndex((s) => s.members.some((m) => m.id === itemId));
  if (index === -1 || index >= visibleCount || slots[index].members.length > 1) return items;
  const target = index + delta;
  if (target < 0 || target >= visibleCount) return items;
  return reorderItemsBySlot(items, slots, slots[index].slotId, slots[target].slotId);
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* EdgeLineMark — the Block's edge itself, drawn as a straight line     */
/* through the track.                                                  */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

// The skin seam (src/skin/skin.ts + skin.css). Ported from `var(--border)`
// to `--bbox-skin-stroke` — the same token Block's own header/footer
// dividers read — with today's value as the fallback, so a consumer who
// never imports skin.css renders the exact line this replaced.
const EDGE_LINE_COLOR = "var(--bbox-skin-stroke, var(--border))";

function EdgeLineMark({ axis }: { axis: "row" | "column" }) {
  return (
    <div
      data-slot="port-edge-center-line"
      aria-hidden
      style={
        axis === "row"
          ? { position: "absolute", left: 0, right: 0, top: "50%", height: 1, background: EDGE_LINE_COLOR, pointerEvents: "none" }
          : { position: "absolute", top: 0, bottom: 0, left: "50%", width: 1, background: EDGE_LINE_COLOR, pointerEvents: "none" }
      }
    />
  );
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* PortEdgeSlotBox — ONE port's content, at a FIXED main-axis size       */
/* (`slotMainPx`) shared by every slot on the edge — single or grouped.  */
/* That shared, content-independent size is what makes B5's pitch match  */
/* provable rather than coincidental: see `interSlotGapPx`'s own doc.    */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

interface PortEdgeSlotBoxProps {
  item: PortEdgeItem | undefined;
  edge: BlockSide;
  showPorts: boolean;
  showCard: boolean;
  slotMainPx: number;
  renderItem?: PortEdgeProps["renderItem"];
  /** Part B: an explicit label placement wins over the edge's own inward
   *  default — the SAME `PortTextLayout` vocabulary `LabeledPort` already
   *  has (Phase 2), reused rather than invented twice. */
  textLayoutOverride?: PortTextLayout;
  /** Phase 4: the lane is open — hide this port's own single-line label
   *  (the dot stays) via `LabeledPort`'s existing `labelSlot` seam, present
   *  since Phase 2 for exactly this kind of swap
   *  (`labelSlot !== undefined` -> present-beats-absent, B4/labeledPort.tsx).
   *  No call site sets this anymore (F-1 below removed the only one) — kept
   *  as a live seam, not deleted, since a host's own `renderItem` override
   *  may still want it. */
  hideLabel?: boolean;
  /**
   * F-1 (docs/FEEDBACK-2026-09-17c-PLAN.md: "I like the toggle but the
   * actual ports should basically look identical. The only difference is
   * when you click the port to edit it."): whether THIS port's own built-in
   * label is click-to-edit right now — forwarded straight to `LabeledPort`'s
   * `disabled` (inverted). `PortEdge`'s `editing` prop is what arms it,
   * edge-wide; unset/false leaves every port exactly as it renders with no
   * `editing` prop at all, which is what makes the toggle's resting state
   * byte-identical on or off (F1a) — nothing here changes what paints until
   * a port is actually clicked.
   */
  editingEnabled?: boolean;
  /**
   * F-1: fired with THIS port's own id on every keystroke of its built-in
   * label editor — the per-port equivalent of the old lane-wide
   * `onItemsChange` rewrite, now scoped to one port so editing it can never
   * touch its neighbours (F1c: "other ports' labels are unchanged").
   * Omitted for a synthesized item (the collapsed "+N more" pseudo-port has
   * no real label to rename).
   */
  onLabelWrite?(id: string, value: string): void;
  /**
   * F-1 continuity: `PortEdgeProps.laneLanguage` reaches whichever editor a
   * click actually opens — this port's own `labelLanguage` in `"single"`
   * mode, `PortLaneOverlay`'s `language` in `"multi"` mode (both wired from
   * the same source prop, since only one of the two ever mounts at once).
   */
  labelLanguage?: LanguageSupport;
  /** `PortEdgeProps.variant`, threaded down the same way `labelLanguage` is. */
  variant?: PortVariantId;
  /** F-1: `PortEdgeProps.editMode`, threaded down the same way `hideLabel`
   *  already was. */
  editMode?: "single" | "multi";
  /**
   * F-1 `"multi"` mode's click target: fired instead of the port's own
   * `onLabelWrite` path when a port is clicked with `editMode: "multi"` —
   * opens the rail-wide lane (`PortEdgeBody`'s own `laneOpen` state) rather
   * than this one port's inline editor. No id/value payload — unlike
   * `onLabelWrite`, this isn't an edit, it's a request to open a DIFFERENT
   * editor that then owns every port's text itself.
   */
  onRequestLaneOpen?(): void;
  /**
   * 2026-09-17 (Lane I, "what does dnd-kit actually use for a grouped
   * family" — see the report; UPDATED 2026-09-18 for W1's
   * `AnchorSlotCard`): a real (not collapsed) group's dnd-kit
   * registration is ONE `useDraggable`/`useDroppable({id: slot.slotId})`
   * pair on the SLOT's own wrapping div (`AnchorSlotCard`/`FreeSlotCard`
   * below) — never one per member, measured live via
   * `useDndContext().droppableRects`. Drawing a
   * `showCard` overlay per MEMBER here (the pre-fix behaviour) therefore
   * drew a rectangle dnd-kit never registers — N small boxes with gaps
   * between them, when the thing actually being dragged/collision-tested is
   * ONE rectangle enclosing the whole family. `GroupSlotBody` sets this to
   * suppress the per-member surface and draws that one rectangle itself,
   * sized to its own container (the same box the wrapping div's `getBoundingClientRect()`
   * — and therefore dnd-kit's own measurement — returns).
   */
  hideCardSurface?: boolean;
  /** `PortEdgeProps.reorderChord`'s key hook, bound by `PortEdgeBody` to
   *  this edge's items; `true` = the key was the chord and is claimed. */
  onReorderKey?(id: string, event: KeyboardEvent): boolean;
  /** The chord hint (`reorderHintText`) shown while THIS port's editor is
   *  focused. Set exactly when `onReorderKey` is. */
  reorderHint?: string;
}

/**
 * Where the chord hint sits: on the dot's OUTWARD side, the one side of a
 * port no label ever flows toward by default (`inwardTextLayout`), so the
 * hint can never cover the text being edited.
 */
const REORDER_HINT_PLACEMENT: Record<BlockSide, CSSProperties> = {
  left: { right: "100%", top: "50%", transform: "translateY(-50%)", marginRight: 2 },
  right: { left: "100%", top: "50%", transform: "translateY(-50%)", marginLeft: 2 },
  top: { bottom: "100%", left: "50%", transform: "translateX(-50%)" },
  bottom: { top: "100%", left: "50%", transform: "translateX(-50%)" },
};

// B3: a slot must occupy its rail position even when nothing renders inside
// it (`showPorts` and `showCard` both off) — a distribution scheme spaces
// BOXES, and a zero-size box breaks the pitch it's supposed to hold.
//
// WHY unconditional now (2026-09-17, docs/FEEDBACK-2026-09-17b-PLAN.md, Lane
// B): this used to be `showCard ? cardMainPx() : EMPTY_SLOT_MIN_PX` — the
// slot literally grew to make room for the card's own border+padding the
// instant `showCard` flipped on, which is the precise mechanism behind "the
// members seem to shrink a bit to fit inside the card" (the port itself
// didn't shrink; the box it shares with every other slot on the edge did,
// dragging the whole edge's pitch with it). The overlay below draws INSIDE
// this same fixed footprint instead of requiring a bigger one.
const EMPTY_SLOT_MIN_PX = 24;
// Chrome DevTools' own element-highlight recipe: translucent fill + a solid
// edge, red because that's what Zach asked for. The skin seam touches only
// SHAPE here (radius, stroke-width) — never colour: this red is a deliberate
// ruling (Zach, 2026-09-17), not a state the skin owns, so it is exempt from
// `--bbox-skin-stroke`/`-surface` the way Badge's tinted variants are exempt
// from `--bbox-skin-line-override`. `rounded-lg`/`border` (Tailwind) below
// is replaced by these two so the fallback literal is provably identical —
// `--radius-lg` IS what `rounded-lg` resolves to, `1px` IS what the bare
// `border` utility sets.
const CARD_OVERLAY_FILL = "rgb(239 68 68 / 0.18)";
const CARD_OVERLAY_BORDER = "rgb(239 68 68 / 0.7)";
const CARD_OVERLAY_RADIUS = "var(--bbox-skin-radius, var(--radius-lg))";
const CARD_OVERLAY_STROKE_WIDTH = "var(--bbox-skin-stroke-width, 1px)";

function PortEdgeSlotBox({ item, edge, showPorts, showCard, slotMainPx, renderItem, textLayoutOverride, hideLabel, hideCardSurface, editingEnabled, onLabelWrite, labelLanguage, editMode = "single", onRequestLaneOpen, variant, onReorderKey, reorderHint }: PortEdgeSlotBoxProps) {
  const textLayout = textLayoutOverride ?? inwardTextLayout(edge);
  // F-1: in `"multi"` mode, a click opens the RAIL's lane, not this port's
  // own inline editor — so `LabeledPort` itself must stay `disabled` (its
  // own click-to-edit never engages) and a capture-phase listener on the
  // member box below does the opening instead, before the click can reach
  // anything else. `"single"` mode (the default) is unchanged: `disabled`
  // is simply the inverse of "armed".
  const singleLineEditable = Boolean(editingEnabled) && editMode === "single";
  const port =
    item == null ? null : renderItem ? (
      renderItem(item, {
        textLayout,
        onReorderKey: onReorderKey ? (event) => onReorderKey(item.id, event) : undefined,
        editable: singleLineEditable,
        onLabelWrite: (value: string) => onLabelWrite?.(item.id, value),
      })
    ) : showPorts ? (
      <LabeledPort
        edge={edge}
        state={item.state ?? "empty"}
        diagnostics={item.diagnostics}
        availability={item.availability}
        // No `diameter` here since 2026-09-19 (was a hard `"md"`): the
        // variant decides the default dot, so the small pair Zach asked
        // for reaches every rail without each edge naming a size.
        variant={variant}
        type={item.type}
        typeColor={item.typeColor}
        producers={item.producers}
        selected={item.selected}
        textLayout={textLayout}
        labelSlot={hideLabel ? null : undefined}
        // F-1: `disabled` is the inverse of "armed for single-line editing"
        // — with no `editing` prop at all (or `editing=false`) this is
        // `disabled`, which is exactly the ordinary, unwired `LabeledPort`
        // default every other story on this file already renders. Armed AND
        // in `"single"` mode, a click opens THIS port's own single-line
        // editor (`labeledPort.tsx`'s `DefaultPortLabel`, F-1's other half,
        // already built) — never a second port's, never a rail-wide
        // overlay. Armed AND in `"multi"` mode, this stays `disabled` too —
        // the capture listener below owns the click instead.
        disabled={!singleLineEditable}
        onLabelWrite={onLabelWrite ? (value) => onLabelWrite(item.id, value) : undefined}
        onLabelKeyDown={onReorderKey ? (event) => onReorderKey(item.id, event) : undefined}
        labelLanguage={labelLanguage}
      >
        {item.label}
      </LabeledPort>
    ) : null;

  const boxStyle: CSSProperties = {
    position: "relative",
    display: "inline-flex",
    alignItems: "center",
    justifyContent: "center",
    width: slotMainPx,
    height: slotMainPx,
    boxSizing: "border-box",
    flexShrink: 0,
    // WHY `top` (lane `ports`, 2026-09-19): this box is INLINE-level inside
    // the sortable card (`display: block`), so by default it sits on the
    // card's text baseline — and an inline-flex box's baseline is its first
    // item's, i.e. the bottom edge of whatever dot is inside. That made the
    // slot's position a function of the DOT'S SIZE: the 12px `md` dot
    // happened to put the box exactly at the card's top, the 6px default dot
    // shifted every slot 3px down and grew the card to 27px (measured:
    // member top 153.5 vs card top 150.5 — every rail pitch 45 instead of
    // 42). Pinning the box to the line's top makes the slot geometry
    // independent of what is drawn inside it, which is the promise
    // `PORT_EDGE_SLOT_MIN_PX` already makes to every layout consumer.
    verticalAlign: "top",
  };

  return (
    <span
      data-slot="port-edge-member"
      style={boxStyle}
      // F-1 `"multi"` mode's click target — capture phase, so it runs
      // BEFORE `LabeledPort`'s own bubble-phase click handler would (moot
      // while `disabled`, but keeps this correct if a future `renderItem`
      // override supplies its own clickable content) and `stopPropagation`
      // keeps one click from also reaching an ancestor's own handlers (the
      // sortable card's drag activation, in particular — a plain click
      // never starts a drag on its own, but there is no reason to let this
      // one bubble further than it needs to).
      onClickCapture={
        item != null && editingEnabled && editMode === "multi" && onRequestLaneOpen
          ? (event) => {
              event.stopPropagation();
              onRequestLaneOpen();
            }
          : undefined
      }
    >
      {port}
      {reorderHint && (
        // Shown only while this port's editor has focus — keyboardReorder.css.
        <span
          data-slot="port-edge-reorder-hint"
          aria-hidden
          style={{
            position: "absolute",
            ...REORDER_HINT_PLACEMENT[edge],
            fontSize: 10,
            lineHeight: 1,
            whiteSpace: "nowrap",
            color: "var(--muted-foreground)",
            pointerEvents: "none",
          }}
        >
          {reorderHint}
        </span>
      )}
      {showCard && !hideCardSurface && (
        // B3: "actually show a card, like a kanban card" — now an overlay,
        // not a wrapper (see EMPTY_SLOT_MIN_PX's own comment): painted OVER
        // the same box `port` already occupies, `absolute inset-0` so it
        // contributes nothing to the box's size, `pointer-events: none` so
        // it can't steal the drag that reorders slots. Renders even with
        // `port` null, so `showCard` alone still reads as a real (if empty)
        // card.
        <div
          aria-hidden
          data-slot="port-edge-card-surface"
          className="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 border"
          style={{
            borderRadius: CARD_OVERLAY_RADIUS,
            borderWidth: CARD_OVERLAY_STROKE_WIDTH,
            backgroundColor: CARD_OVERLAY_FILL,
            borderColor: CARD_OVERLAY_BORDER,
          }}
        />
      )}
    </span>
  );
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* Slot body — the ×N chip once collapsed (B8: unchanged); otherwise a   */
/* single PortEdgeSlotBox, or — a real "grouped" family, not collapsed — */
/* every member's own box laid out along the edge's OWN axis (B7) at the */
/* SAME pitch the rest of the edge uses (B5), with one enclosing outline */
/* around the group's circles (B6).                                     */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

interface SlotRenderProps {
  slot: Slot;
  axis: "row" | "column";
  edge: BlockSide;
  itemsById: Map<string, PortEdgeItem>;
  showPorts: boolean;
  showCard: boolean;
  collapsed: boolean;
  slotMainPx: number;
  gapPx: number;
  color?: string;
  renderItem?: PortEdgeProps["renderItem"];
  /** D-A7 — see `PortEdgeProps.renderCollapsed`. */
  renderCollapsed?: PortEdgeProps["renderCollapsed"];
  textLayoutOverride?: PortTextLayout;
  hideLabel?: boolean;
  /** F-1 — see `PortEdgeSlotBoxProps`'s own doc; threaded through the same
   *  way `hideLabel` already was. */
  editingEnabled?: boolean;
  onLabelWrite?(id: string, value: string): void;
  labelLanguage?: LanguageSupport;
  variant?: PortVariantId;
  editMode?: "single" | "multi";
  onRequestLaneOpen?(): void;
  /** `PortEdgeProps.reorderChord`, bound — see `PortEdgeSlotBoxProps`. */
  onReorderKey?(id: string, event: KeyboardEvent): boolean;
  reorderHint?: string;
}

const GROUP_OUTLINE_PAD = 6;

/**
 * B6: measured, not computed — the group's own members are real flex
 * children (B7's axis-aware stack), so there's no closed-form box for
 * "wherever their circles actually landed" the way there is for the pitch
 * (whose formula this file derives precisely because a closed form exists
 * there). Scoped to ONE group's own DOM subtree — simpler and cheaper than
 * a track-wide sweep, and correct by construction since a group's members
 * are always each other's DOM siblings here.
 */
function useEnclosingCircleBox(containerRef: RefObject<HTMLDivElement | null>, deps: unknown[]) {
  const [box, setBox] = useState<{ left: number; top: number; width: number; height: number } | null>(null);
  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    const el = containerRef.current;
    if (!el) {
      setBox(null);
      return;
    }
    const dots = Array.from(el.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-slot="port-dot"]'));
    if (dots.length < 2) {
      setBox(null);
      return;
    }
    const containerRect = el.getBoundingClientRect();
    const rects = dots.map((d) => d.getBoundingClientRect());
    const left = Math.min(...rects.map((r) => r.left)) - containerRect.left - GROUP_OUTLINE_PAD;
    const top = Math.min(...rects.map((r) => r.top)) - containerRect.top - GROUP_OUTLINE_PAD;
    const right = Math.max(...rects.map((r) => r.right)) - containerRect.left + GROUP_OUTLINE_PAD;
    const bottom = Math.max(...rects.map((r) => r.bottom)) - containerRect.top + GROUP_OUTLINE_PAD;
    setBox({ left, top, width: right - left, height: bottom - top });
    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
  }, deps);
  return box;
}

function GroupSlotBody({ slot, edge, itemsById, showPorts, showCard, axis, slotMainPx, gapPx, color, renderItem, textLayoutOverride, hideLabel, editingEnabled, onLabelWrite, labelLanguage, editMode, onRequestLaneOpen, variant, onReorderKey, reorderHint }: SlotRenderProps) {
  const containerRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement | null>(null);
  const memberIds = slot.members.map((m) => m.id).join(",");
  const box = useEnclosingCircleBox(containerRef, [memberIds, axis, showPorts, showCard, slotMainPx, gapPx]);
  return (
    <div data-slot="port-edge-group" ref={containerRef} style={{ position: "relative", display: "flex", flexDirection: axis === "row" ? "row" : "column", gap: gapPx }}>
      {box && (
        // The skin seam (src/skin/skin.ts + skin.css) — same recipe
        // Block/Badge/Port already use: the CSS border's COLOUR is state-
        // owned (`color`, the group's own accent, same split as Port's own
        // ring — see traceTerminal.tsx's identical reasoning), so it reads
        // `--bbox-skin-line-override` rather than `--bbox-skin-stroke`;
        // width and radius are the skin's to set outright, same as every
        // other bordered box in this port. WHY no drawn twin lives here any
        // more (docs/peps/0001-sketch-skin-at-the-boundary.md M5): this
        // used to render a `SketchOutline` child always, with CSS deciding
        // which of the two painted — the arrangement the ruling retires.
        // This `position: absolute` box still establishes the containing
        // block a drawn twin needs (the same recipe `block.tsx` uses); the
        // boundary pass now appends into it directly, selecting on
        // `[data-slot="port-edge-group-outline"]` in `BOX_SELECTOR`
        // (`sketchAtEdge.ts`) rather than being told to by a child element.
        <div
          data-slot="port-edge-group-outline"
          data-group-id={slot.slotId}
          // WHY: docs/peps/0001-sketch-skin-at-the-boundary.md M7. This
          // component's own seed (`port-edge-group:${slot.slotId}`) — the
          // edge's geometry fallback would otherwise reuse `${w}x${h}`,
          // which two differently-membered groups of the same enclosing
          // size would collide on.
          data-bbox-seed={`port-edge-group:${slot.slotId}`}
          aria-hidden
          style={{
            position: "absolute",
            left: box.left,
            top: box.top,
            width: box.width,
            height: box.height,
            border: `var(--bbox-skin-stroke-width, 2px) solid var(--bbox-skin-line-override, ${color ?? "var(--border)"})`,
            borderRadius: "var(--bbox-skin-radius, 10px)",
            pointerEvents: "none",
            // WHY: docs/peps/0001-sketch-skin-at-the-boundary.md M6. Same
            // reasoning as `port.tsx`'s ring — this box's accent colour is
            // STATE-owned (`color`, the group's own accent), so it is
            // published in a channel the sketch skin never blanks, rather
            // than left for a boundary pass to read back a `border-color`
            // the skin has already made transparent.
            ...({ "--bbox-ink-state": color ?? "var(--border)" } as CSSProperties),
          }}
        />
      )}
      {showCard && (
        // "What does dnd-kit actually use for a grouped family" (Lane I,
        // UPDATED 2026-09-18 for W1's `AnchorSlotCard`): ONE rectangle,
        // registered by `AnchorSlotCard`/`FreeSlotCard`'s own
        // `useDraggable`/`useDroppable({id: slot.slotId})` pair on the div
        // that wraps THIS component whole — measured live via
        // `useDndContext().droppableRects`
        // (see the report), not the N per-member boxes the pre-fix overlay
        // drew. `inset-0` of THIS `position:relative` container is exactly
        // that wrapping div's own box (no padding/border sits between them),
        // so the drawn rectangle and the one dnd-kit registers are the same
        // rectangle by construction, not by coincidence — unlike the
        // `port-edge-group-outline` above (deliberately padded around the
        // dots for legibility), this one must NOT add `GROUP_OUTLINE_PAD` or
        // it would stop matching what dnd-kit measures.
        <div
          aria-hidden
          data-slot="port-edge-card-surface"
          className="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 border"
          style={{
            borderRadius: CARD_OVERLAY_RADIUS,
            borderWidth: CARD_OVERLAY_STROKE_WIDTH,
            backgroundColor: CARD_OVERLAY_FILL,
            borderColor: CARD_OVERLAY_BORDER,
          }}
        />
      )}
      {slot.members.map((m) => (
        <PortEdgeSlotBox
          key={m.id}
          item={itemsById.get(m.id)}
          edge={edge}
          showPorts={showPorts}
          showCard={showCard}
          slotMainPx={slotMainPx}
          renderItem={renderItem}
          textLayoutOverride={textLayoutOverride}
          hideLabel={hideLabel}
          editingEnabled={editingEnabled}
          onLabelWrite={onLabelWrite}
          labelLanguage={labelLanguage}
          variant={variant}
          editMode={editMode}
          onRequestLaneOpen={onRequestLaneOpen}
          // A grouped member claims the chord too, so it never falls
          // through to a word-jump on one port and a move on its
          // neighbour — `moveItemOneSlot` then leaves the family as-is.
          onReorderKey={onReorderKey}
          reorderHint={reorderHint}
          hideCardSurface
        />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}

function SlotBody(props: SlotRenderProps) {
  const { slot, itemsById, collapsed } = props;
  const isRealGroup = slot.members.length > 1;

  // Follow-up feedback item 5: collapsed renders ONE real port — not a
  // bare pill with no dot — standing in for the whole group, its label
  // replaced with "+N more" (N = the group's full member count). Chosen
  // idiom: "+N more", the SAME wording item 6's hidden-port count uses,
  // rather than shipping two idioms (`×N` here, "+X more" there) for what
  // Zach called "the same fact". No member is named individually once
  // collapsed, so N counts the WHOLE cluster, not "besides the one shown"
  // — the dot is a visual anchor (it borrows the first member's own state,
  // so a collapsed group of all-`wired` ports still reads as wired), not a
  // stand-in for that specific port's identity.
  //
  // Reusing `PortEdgeSlotBox` verbatim — rather than a bespoke pill — is
  // what keeps B8's "fits a single card slot" guarantee true BY
  // CONSTRUCTION: it gets the exact same `slotMainPx` box every other slot
  // uses, so there is nothing new to measure.
  if (collapsed && isRealGroup) {
    const first = slot.members[0];
    const sourceItem = first ? itemsById.get(first.id) : undefined;
    const collapsedItem: PortEdgeItem = {
      id: slot.slotId,
      label: `+${slot.members.length} more`,
      state: sourceItem?.state ?? "empty",
    };
    // D-A7: the whole family, in the order it renders — what
    // `renderCollapsed` is handed, and the reason it is a different seam
    // from `renderItem` (which only ever sees one port).
    const members = slot.members
      .map((m) => itemsById.get(m.id))
      .filter((it): it is PortEdgeItem => it != null);
    return (
      <PortEdgeSlotBox
        item={collapsedItem}
        edge={props.edge}
        showPorts={props.showPorts}
        showCard={props.showCard}
        slotMainPx={props.slotMainPx}
        // Deliberately NOT `props.renderItem` — a host override renders
        // ONE port's real content; the collapsed summary is a fixed idiom
        // of this component's own, same as before this change.
        //
        // D-A7: `props.renderCollapsed` IS consulted, because it is given
        // the whole family instead. Absent, the `+N more` chip above stands
        // unchanged — this branch is byte-identical without the prop.
        renderItem={props.renderCollapsed ? (_item, ctx) => props.renderCollapsed?.(members, ctx) : undefined}
        textLayoutOverride={props.textLayoutOverride}
        hideLabel={props.hideLabel}
        variant={props.variant}
        // F-1: deliberately NOT forwarding `editingEnabled`/`onLabelWrite`
        // here — `collapsedItem` is a synthesized "+N more" pseudo-port with
        // no real label of its own; wiring a write handler to it would let a
        // click rename the SUMMARY text and silently corrupt whichever real
        // member happens to share `collapsedItem.id` (it doesn't — the id is
        // the slot's, not any member's — so today it would just write into a
        // dangling id instead). Collapsed stays view-only, same as before.
      />
    );
  }

  // B5/B7: a real, NOT-collapsed group renders every member's own box,
  // along the edge's own axis, at the edge's own pitch.
  if (isRealGroup) return <GroupSlotBody {...props} />;

  const member = slot.members[0];
  const item = member ? itemsById.get(member.id) : undefined;
  return (
    <PortEdgeSlotBox
      item={item}
      edge={props.edge}
      showPorts={props.showPorts}
      showCard={props.showCard}
      slotMainPx={props.slotMainPx}
      renderItem={props.renderItem}
      textLayoutOverride={props.textLayoutOverride}
      hideLabel={props.hideLabel}
      editingEnabled={props.editingEnabled}
      onLabelWrite={props.onLabelWrite}
      labelLanguage={props.labelLanguage}
      variant={props.variant}
      editMode={props.editMode}
      onRequestLaneOpen={props.onRequestLaneOpen}
      onReorderKey={props.onReorderKey}
      reorderHint={props.reorderHint}
    />
  );
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* Cards — anchored (Auto mode) and free-draggable (Custom mode).       */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

/**
 * AnchorSlotCard — W1's replacement for the old `SortableSlotCard`
 * (`useSortable` + a flex/`SortableContext` track). Comment B1 and
 * `neutralizeScale`, deleted with it, existed only because
 * `@dnd-kit/sortable`'s `useDerivedTransform` computes a SIBLING'S
 * transform from measured rect deltas as a live reorder shifts its
 * index, and that derived transform can carry a scale that visibly
 * squeezed a card for a frame. This card has no such derived transform
 * to neutralize: `left`/`top` come straight from `PortEdgeBody`'s own
 * anchor computation (a registered `PortLayoutFn`, run over the tentative
 * order while a drag is over this rail — see that component's own doc),
 * and `useDraggable`'s `transform` is nothing but this card's OWN pointer
 * delta, never a sibling's. One geometry owner makes B1's bug class
 * impossible instead of neutralized.
 *
 * Both draggable (so it can be picked up) and droppable (so `nearestAnchor`
 * can name it as a drop target) at the SAME id — the manual-sortable
 * pattern `@dnd-kit/core`'s own docs describe for a custom
 * `collisionDetection`, used here instead of `useSortable` precisely
 * because `useSortable` is what carried the B1 coupling.
 *
 * WHY (Zach, 2026-09-18, quoted in commit 76e3b92's message): "the port
 * layout things which support arbitrary layouts and then DND kit basically
 * just allows us to kind of like modify the data structure." `arrayMove`
 * on drop is dnd-kit's only write; where a card SITS is the layout
 * function's answer, never a transform dnd-kit derived. Do not bring
 * `useSortable` back for its settle animation — that re-opens B1.
 *
 * `fixed` — an `anchored` distribution's card (`AnchoredConfig`, ported
 * from claude/notebook-lab-work-order-472c22 `27b4314`, where it was a
 * second component, `AnchoredSlotCard`). Same position, same body, but no
 * gesture: the drag and drop registrations are `disabled` (dnd-kit then
 * hands back no `listeners`, and `nearestAnchor` never sees this card as
 * a target), no `attributes` (no `role="button"`/tab stop on a dot a
 * cable may later drag from), no grab cursor and no `touchAction`
 * override, so the dot keeps whatever pointer behaviour its own content
 * declares. WHY one card with a flag, not the donor's second component:
 * the positioning formula is the one thing both must share byte-for-byte
 * — a host reasons about one mode from the other only if the same number
 * lands on the same pixel — and the hooks stay unconditional, so an edge
 * that switches distribution at runtime keeps each card's hook order.
 */
function AnchorSlotCard(props: SlotRenderProps & { left: number; top: number; fixed?: boolean }) {
  const { left, top, fixed = false, ...slotProps } = props;
  const { attributes, listeners, setNodeRef: setDragRef, transform, isDragging } = useDraggable({ id: props.slot.slotId, disabled: fixed });
  const { setNodeRef: setDropRef } = useDroppable({ id: props.slot.slotId, disabled: fixed });
  const setRefs = useCallback(
    (el: HTMLDivElement | null) => {
      setDragRef(el);
      setDropRef(el);
    },
    [setDragRef, setDropRef],
  );
  const drag = CSS.Translate.toString(transform);
  const style: CSSProperties = {
    position: "absolute",
    left,
    top,
    transform: drag || undefined,
    // `dragActive` is the one place a gesture leaks into paint order —
    // same rule `FreeSlotCard` already follows, below.
    zIndex: isDragging ? paintTier("dragActive") : paintTier("memberContent"),
    opacity: isDragging ? 0.35 : 1,
    ...(fixed ? {} : { cursor: "grab", touchAction: "none" }),
    // Re-arms what `blockPort.tsx`'s rail wrapper (`block-port-rail`) turns
    // off at the box level — this card is the one thing in a slot's box
    // that IS real content (a port, draggable), so it alone opts back into
    // hit-testing. See that file's own WHY on the rail style.
    pointerEvents: "auto",
  };
  return (
    <div
      ref={setRefs}
      data-slot="port-edge-card"
      data-anchored={fixed ? "" : undefined}
      data-dragging={isDragging || undefined}
      style={style}
      {...(fixed ? {} : attributes)}
      {...listeners}
    >
      <SlotBody {...slotProps} />
    </div>
  );
}

function FreeSlotCard(props: SlotRenderProps & { axis: "row" | "column"; x: number; shift: number }) {
  // WHY dnd-kit's own `transform` is NOT applied: it follows the pointer on
  // BOTH axes, with no order or gap. `x + shift` is already the constrained
  // live position (`PortEdgeBody` feeds the drag delta through
  // `constrainPortPositions` every move), so the card moves along its own
  // wall only and stops where the rules stop it — what you see mid-drag is
  // exactly what the drop commits.
  //
  // WHY the dragged card's live travel is a `shift` (a transform) and not
  // folded into `x` (its `top`/`left`): dnd-kit measures the active node
  // transform-agnostically and treats any LAYOUT movement of it as the page
  // shifting under the pointer, subtracting it from the drag delta. Moving
  // the dragged card's `top` therefore fed straight back into the next delta
  // — measured: the delta oscillated 20, 0, 20, 0 on every frame and the
  // port never left its slot. Neighbours are not the active node, so a
  // pushed neighbour moves by `x` alone.
  const { attributes, listeners, setNodeRef, isDragging } = useDraggable({ id: props.slot.slotId });
  const style: CSSProperties = {
    position: "absolute",
    ...(props.axis === "row"
      ? { left: props.x + CONTAINER_PADDING, top: "50%", transform: `translate(${props.shift}px, -50%)` }
      : { top: props.x + CONTAINER_PADDING, left: "50%", transform: `translate(-50%, ${props.shift}px)` }),
    // `dragActive` is the one place a gesture leaks into paint order —
    // named explicitly (paintOrder.ts) rather than left as an inline
    // ternary, per the brief's own instruction.
    zIndex: isDragging ? paintTier("dragActive") : paintTier("memberContent"),
    opacity: isDragging ? 0.6 : 1,
    cursor: "grab",
    touchAction: "none",
    // See `AnchorSlotCard`'s identical line — re-arms hit-testing that
    // `blockPort.tsx`'s rail wrapper turns off at the box level.
    pointerEvents: "auto",
  };
  return (
    <div ref={setNodeRef} data-slot="port-edge-card" data-slot-id={props.slot.slotId} data-dragging={isDragging || undefined} style={style} {...attributes} {...listeners}>
      <SlotBody {...props} />
    </div>
  );
}

/**
 * The drag-time "min space" marker, between two ports held at exactly the
 * minimum gap. Zach's sketch draws it as a red bracket labelled "Min space
 * requirement"; here it is a muted bracket on the wall's OUTWARD side (where
 * no label flows — `inwardTextLayout`), spanning dot centre to dot centre,
 * and it only exists while a drag is in flight.
 */
function MinGapMarker({ edge, axis, from, to, crossPx }: { edge: BlockSide; axis: "row" | "column"; from: number; to: number; crossPx: number }) {
  const OUTWARD_PX = 10;
  const TICK_PX = 4;
  const line = "1px solid var(--muted-foreground)";
  const cross = crossPx / 2 - CONTAINER_BORDER + (edge === "left" || edge === "top" ? -OUTWARD_PX - TICK_PX : OUTWARD_PX);
  const style: CSSProperties =
    axis === "column"
      ? {
          top: CONTAINER_PADDING + from,
          height: to - from,
          left: cross,
          width: TICK_PX,
          borderTop: line,
          borderBottom: line,
          ...(edge === "left" ? { borderLeft: line } : { borderRight: line }),
        }
      : {
          left: CONTAINER_PADDING + from,
          width: to - from,
          top: cross,
          height: TICK_PX,
          borderLeft: line,
          borderRight: line,
          ...(edge === "top" ? { borderTop: line } : { borderBottom: line }),
        };
  return (
    <span
      data-slot="port-edge-min-gap"
      aria-hidden
      style={{ position: "absolute", boxSizing: "border-box", opacity: 0.7, pointerEvents: "none", zIndex: paintTier("memberContent"), ...style }}
    />
  );
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* Phase 4 — the multi-line lane. Availability predicate, pitch/origin  */
/* measurement (D8), and the CodeEditor overlay itself.                */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

/**
 * Whether the multi-line lane can open, AND why not when it can't — a
 * disabled control must say why (PORT-EDGE-PLAN-PHASE4.md §5: never hide a
 * present feature; a silent no-op violates truthful rendering outright).
 * Column edges only (a CodeMirror document's lines stack vertically);
 * `mode="auto"` only (Custom's author-dragged positions have no uniform
 * pitch for the lane's lines to sit on);
 * blocked ONLY when a `groupMode==="collapsed"` REAL family would render as
 * a ×N chip — one row standing in for N ports breaks the line<->dot
 * bijection.
 *
 * `groupMode==="grouped"` does NOT block, and this is the load-bearing
 * finding the coordinator asked this file to settle: B5/B7's fix (this
 * file's own `GroupSlotBody`) means a grouped-but-not-collapsed family
 * still renders every member's own dot, at the SAME uniform pitch the rest
 * of the edge uses (`interSlotGapPx`) — so the line<->dot bijection holds
 * there too, and PORT-EDGE-PLAN-PHASE4.md's A11 defensive gate
 * ("grouped-with-no-real-family stays enabled") generalizes cleanly to
 * "collapsed is the only thing that ever blocks it, and only when a real
 * family exists" — checked over the COMPUTED slots, never the raw flag,
 * exactly as A11 already required. Nothing further needs guarding.
 */
export function laneAvailability(
  axis: "row" | "column",
  mode: PortEdgeArrangeMode,
  groupMode: PortEdgeGroupMode,
  slots: Slot[],
): { available: boolean; reason?: string } {
  if (axis !== "column") return { available: false, reason: "a lane needs a vertical edge" };
  if (mode !== "auto") return { available: false, reason: "switch to Auto to edit as lines" };
  const blocked = groupMode === "collapsed" && slots.some((s) => s.members.length > 1);
  if (blocked) return { available: false, reason: "ungroup to edit as lines" };
  return { available: true };
}

// D8: `PORT_ROW_PITCH_PX` (packages/bbox-ui/src/port.layout.ts:50) exists
// with zero call sites anywhere — re-derived as a same-valued FALLBACK
// rather than imported, since the real pitch here is whatever this edge's
// own distribution actually produced (varies with label placement and
// distribution mode — the whole reason Part B's cross-product needs a
// MEASURED pitch, not one formula per combination).
const LANE_PITCH_FALLBACK_PX = 44;
// Follow-up feedback (item 1): the lane now keeps codeEditor.css's own
// frame — `border: 1px solid; padding: 4px 6px; border-radius: 6px` — so it
// reads as a real CodeEditor panel (Zach: "make sure to highlight the
// perimeter so that it looks like a code editor component"), instead of
// zeroing it to bare floating text. The origin math has to cancel that
// REAL inset now, plus the small residual measured live in the real
// browser: every rendered `.cm-line`'s centre sat 2px below the dot it
// should align with, uniformly, across every configuration tried — most
// likely CodeMirror's own base theme contributing a little vertical
// padding inside `.cm-line` that this app's CSS never targets. This is the
// exact defect PORT-EDGE-PLAN-PHASE4.md §3 warns SystemSketch's own lane
// shipped with (there, from a 2px accent border never subtracted) — same
// SYMPTOM, different cause; A1's own measurement is what caught it here
// instead of shipping it silent.
const LANE_FRAME_BORDER_PX = 1;
const LANE_FRAME_PADDING_TOP_PX = 4;
const LANE_CM_RESIDUAL_PX = 2;
const LANE_GAP_PX = PORT_LABEL_GAP + PORT_RING_OVERHANG_PX; // same gap a port's own label sits at.

interface LaneGeometry {
  pitch: number;
  top: number;
  dotLeft: number;
  dotRight: number;
}

/**
 * D8: measure, don't assume. `apps/storybook-basics` is an app, not the
 * core (bbox-ui's one rule is the CORE never measuring itself), so reading
 * the live dots' own rendered positions is legal here — and it's the only
 * way to stay correct across Part B's full cross-product (labels left /
 * right / offset × aligned-to-edge / evenly-spaced): the pitch is
 * label-height- and distribution-driven, not a constant.
 */
function useLaneGeometry(trackRef: RefObject<HTMLDivElement | null>, active: boolean, deps: unknown[]): LaneGeometry | null {
  const [geometry, setGeometry] = useState<LaneGeometry | null>(null);
  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    if (!active) {
      setGeometry(null);
      return;
    }
    const track = trackRef.current;
    if (!track) return;
    const measure = () => {
      const dots = Array.from(track.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-slot="port-dot"]'));
      if (dots.length === 0) {
        setGeometry(null);
        return;
      }
      const trackRect = track.getBoundingClientRect();
      const firstRect = dots[0].getBoundingClientRect();
      const secondRect = dots.length >= 2 ? dots[1].getBoundingClientRect() : null;
      const pitch = secondRect ? secondRect.top + secondRect.height / 2 - (firstRect.top + firstRect.height / 2) : LANE_PITCH_FALLBACK_PX;
      const firstCenterY = firstRect.top + firstRect.height / 2 - trackRect.top;
      const top = firstCenterY - pitch / 2 - LANE_FRAME_BORDER_PX - LANE_FRAME_PADDING_TOP_PX - LANE_CM_RESIDUAL_PX;
      setGeometry({ pitch, top, dotLeft: firstRect.left - trackRect.left, dotRight: firstRect.right - trackRect.left });
    };
    measure();
    const ro = new ResizeObserver(measure);
    ro.observe(track);
    return () => ro.disconnect();
    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
  }, deps);
  return geometry;
}

let laneIdCounter = 0;

function PortLaneOverlay({
  trackRef,
  visualOrder,
  onItemsChange,
  onClose,
  language,
  dividerAfter,
  onDividerAfterChange,
}: {
  trackRef: RefObject<HTMLDivElement | null>;
  visualOrder: PortEdgeItem[];
  onItemsChange?(items: PortEdgeItem[]): void;
  onClose(): void;
  language?: LanguageSupport;
  dividerAfter?: number[];
  onDividerAfterChange?(next: number[]): void;
}) {
  const geometry = useLaneGeometry(trackRef, true, [visualOrder.map((p) => p.id).join(",")]);
  if (!geometry) return null;
  // "Multiple returns": divider-aware format/reconcile only when a host
  // actually wired `onDividerAfterChange` — see `PortEdgeProps.dividerAfter`'s
  // own doc for why that is the gate rather than `dividerAfter` alone.
  const dividerAware = onDividerAfterChange !== undefined;
  const dividerAfterValue = dividerAfter ?? [];
  // Follow-up feedback (item 2): the lane ALWAYS sits to the right of the
  // rail, text left-aligned — regardless of whether the individual ports'
  // OWN labels flow left, right, or offset. Zach: "nobody types
  // comfortably into right-aligned text." PORT-EDGE-PLAN-PHASE4.md §3
  // already recorded this (citing the donor's own `inlineBlockEditing.ts`:
  // "a right-aligned editor feels reverse... parks the lane outside the
  // right edge left-aligned instead") and its own prop table already said
  // `align: "left"` on BOTH sides — this file's earlier version read that
  // as "always align left" but still let `textLayout` steer which SIDE the
  // panel opened on, which is the one thing the spec's own citation was
  // actually about. Position is no longer a function of the resting
  // labels' placement at all.
  const wrapperStyle: CSSProperties = {
    position: "absolute",
    top: geometry.top,
    left: geometry.dotRight + LANE_GAP_PX,
    zIndex: paintTier("laneOverlay"),
    // Same re-arm as `AnchorSlotCard`/`FreeSlotCard` — the lane is a real
    // CodeEditor a user types into, so it opts back into hit-testing that
    // `blockPort.tsx`'s rail wrapper turns off at the box level.
    pointerEvents: "auto",
  };
  return (
    <div data-slot="port-edge-lane" style={wrapperStyle}>
      <CodeEditor
        value={dividerAware ? formatRailLane(visualOrder, dividerAfterValue) : formatPortLane(visualOrder)}
        onWrite={(text) => {
          const mint = (label: string) => ({ id: `lane-${laneIdCounter++}`, label, state: "empty" as const });
          if (dividerAware) {
            const result = reconcileRailLane(visualOrder, dividerAfterValue, text, mint);
            if (result.ports !== visualOrder) onItemsChange?.(result.ports);
            if (!sameDividers(result.dividerAfter, dividerAfterValue)) onDividerAfterChange?.(result.dividerAfter);
            return;
          }
          const next = reconcilePortLane(visualOrder, text, mint);
          if (next !== visualOrder) onItemsChange?.(next);
        }}
        multiline
        lineHeightPx={geometry.pitch}
        align="left"
        language={language}
        autoFocus
        onEnter={onClose}
        onEscape={onClose}
        // Follow-up feedback (item 1): NO LONGER zeroes the sibling
        // `CodeEditor`'s own frame chrome — codeEditor.css's stock
        // `.bbox-code-editor` (bordered, rounded, padded, card background)
        // is exactly "a code editor component", and its existing
        // `:focus-within { border-color: var(--color-primary) }` rule is
        // what paints the focused/active perimeter for free the moment
        // `autoFocus` takes effect — no new CSS, no second visual language.
        // `--color-primary` is redefined to `--color-ring` LOCALLY, as a
        // CSS custom property on this one instance's inline style, rather
        // than editing codeEditor.css itself (that file is the sibling
        // lane's, not touched here): measured live, this app's default
        // shadcn theme gives `--color-primary` the exact same value as
        // `--color-border` (`oklch(0.922 0 0)`), so the stock rule fired
        // correctly but was invisible. `--ring`/`--color-ring`
        // (`oklch(0.708 0 0)`) is this app's OWN existing token for
        // exactly this "focused" concept — `EditableText` already uses
        // `var(--bbox-ring, currentColor)` for its own focus ring — so
        // this reuses an established token rather than inventing a colour.
        // `width` stays an override (content-hugging, not `100%` of an
        // absolutely positioned parent with no opposite edge set).
        style={{ width: "max-content", "--color-primary": "var(--color-ring)" } as CSSProperties}
      />
    </div>
  );
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* useMoreIndicatorPosition — maxVisible's own "+N more" line. Measures    */
/* the real DOM rather than re-deriving flex math (D8's own "measure,      */
/* don't assume" — `useEnclosingCircleBox`/`useLaneGeometry` already do    */
/* exactly this), so it stays correct across every Distribution/axis       */
/* combination without a second formula to keep in sync with               */
/* `mainAxisPositions`/`distributionStyle`.                                 */
/*                                                                          */
/* WHY a HOOK called from `PortEdgeBody` itself, not a separate child      */
/* component reading `trackRef` (`PortLaneOverlay`'s own shape, which this */
/* first mirrored): a callback ref's attachment and a DESCENDANT's own     */
/* `useLayoutEffect` commit in the same pass, children-before-parent — so  */
/* a child sitting UNDER the ref-owning div can observe `trackRef.current` */
/* as still `null` on the very render it and the div both first mount      */
/* (measured live: logged `null` every time `maxVisible` was already set   */
/* on first render, since nothing then makes the effect ever re-fire with  */
/* the ref populated). `PortLaneOverlay` never hits this in practice only  */
/* because `editing` starts `false` — it always mounts on a LATER render,  */
/* by which point the div's ref is already attached from a prior commit.   */
/* `maxVisible` has no such gate (it can be true from the first paint), so */
/* it needs the ordering that's actually guaranteed: a hook called INSIDE  */
/* the same component that owns the ref (`PortEdgeBody`, exactly           */
/* `useEnclosingCircleBox`'s own shape in `GroupSlotBody`) sits ABOVE the  */
/* div in the fiber tree, so its layout effect always commits AFTER the    */
/* div's — the ref is guaranteed attached by then, mount or update alike.  */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

/** The gap between the last visible port's box and the overflow marker,
 *  on whichever axis the edge runs. Exported so a story (or an `editor/`
 *  chip) asserts placement against the component's OWN number rather than
 *  a literal copied into a test. */
export const PORT_EDGE_MORE_GAP_PX = 6;

function useMoreIndicatorPosition(trackRef: RefObject<HTMLDivElement | null>, axis: "row" | "column", hiddenCount: number) {
  const [pos, setPos] = useState<{ left: number; top: number } | null>(null);
  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    if (hiddenCount <= 0) {
      setPos(null);
      return;
    }
    const track = trackRef.current;
    if (!track) {
      setPos(null);
      return;
    }
    const measure = () => {
      const members = Array.from(track.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-slot="port-edge-member"]'));
      const last = members[members.length - 1];
      if (!last) {
        setPos(null);
        return;
      }
      const trackRect = track.getBoundingClientRect();
      const lastRect = last.getBoundingClientRect();
      // WHY the two `client*` terms: `getBoundingClientRect()` returns the
      // BORDER box, but CSS `left`/`top` on an absolutely positioned child
      // are measured from the containing block's PADDING box. The track
      // draws a `CONTAINER_BORDER`-wide border (2px, whenever
      // `showContainer` is on), so the naive difference placed the marker
      // exactly one border-width down and to the right of the port it is
      // supposed to sit beside. Found by measuring the real chip against
      // `PORT_EDGE_MORE_GAP_PX` in `editor/PortOverflowChip.stories.tsx`;
      // the older assertion in `PortEdge.stories.tsx`'s `MaxVisible` used a
      // ±2px tolerance, which is precisely the size of the error, so it
      // reported green. `clientLeft`/`clientTop` ARE the border widths, so
      // this stays correct if the border ever changes or is switched off.
      const originX = trackRect.left + track.clientLeft;
      const originY = trackRect.top + track.clientTop;
      setPos(
        axis === "column"
          ? { left: lastRect.left - originX, top: lastRect.bottom - originY + PORT_EDGE_MORE_GAP_PX }
          : { left: lastRect.right - originX + PORT_EDGE_MORE_GAP_PX, top: lastRect.top - originY + lastRect.height / 2 },
      );
    };
    measure();
    const ro = new ResizeObserver(measure);
    ro.observe(track);
    return () => ro.disconnect();
    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
  }, [trackRef, axis, hiddenCount]);
  return pos;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* PortEdgeBody — the track: droppable + anchored, no opinion about     */
/* who owns the surrounding DndContext.                                */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

function PortEdgeBody({
  id,
  edge,
  items,
  onItemsChange,
  distribution = DEFAULT_DISTRIBUTION,
  mode = "auto",
  freeOffsets = EMPTY_OFFSETS,
  onFreeOffsetsChange,
  customNeighbour = "stop",
  minPortGap = MIN_PORT_GAP_PX,
  groups = "",
  groupMode = "off",
  showPorts = true,
  showCard = false,
  showContainer = true,
  showCenterLine = true,
  size,
  renderItem,
  renderCollapsed,
  renderOverflow,
  textLayout,
  editing = false,
  onEditingChange,
  // RULING 11: the multi-line lane is the DEFAULT editor an armed port
  // click opens. See `PortEdgeProps.editMode` for Zach's own words and for
  // why the `lane.available` fallback below makes this safe everywhere.
  editMode = "multi",
  laneOpen: laneOpenProp,
  onLaneOpenChange,
  laneLanguage,
  maxVisible,
  variant,
  reorderChord,
  dividerAfter,
  onDividerAfterChange,
  anchorLayout,
}: PortEdgeProps) {
  const containerId = id ?? edge;
  const axis = AXIS_FOR_EDGE[edge];
  const resolvedSize = size ?? DEFAULT_SIZE[axis];

  const itemIds = items.map((i) => i.id);
  const itemsById = new Map(items.map((i) => [i.id, i] as const));
  const { set: groupingSet, unknownIds } = groupingSetFromText(groups, itemIds);
  const allSlots = computeSlots(itemIds, groupingSet, groupMode);
  const colorMap = buildColorMap(itemIds, groupingSet);

  // `maxVisible` cuts SLOTS (the rendered boxes), not raw items — a cut
  // through a real (not collapsed) group hides every member of whichever
  // slot falls past the cutoff, and `hiddenPortCount` sums those members'
  // real count rather than counting 1 per cut slot, so the "+N more" text
  // stays a true port count even when the cut lands inside a group.
  //
  // RULING 7: the cut itself is a PURE function (`splitByMaxVisible`), so
  // the count can be unit-tested — and re-derived by nobody — without
  // mounting anything. See `portEdge.layout.test.ts`.
  const { visible: slots, hidden: hiddenSlots, hiddenPortCount } = splitByMaxVisible(allSlots, maxVisible);
  // RULING 7's other half of the payload: WHICH ports fell past the cut, in
  // track order. Resolved here rather than inside the chrome, for the same
  // reason the count is — the chrome is handed the answer, it never goes
  // looking for one.
  const hiddenItems = hiddenSlots.flatMap((slot) => slot.members.map((m) => itemsById.get(m.id)).filter((it): it is PortEdgeItem => it != null));

  // Still what a HOST's own toggle button should read for its
  // disabled/reason state (both `PortEdge.stories.tsx` and
  // `BlockPortLane.stories.tsx` already do) — the multi-line lane's own
  // preconditions (column edge, auto mode, no collapsed real family) are
  // real and unchanged. Per-port SINGLE-line click-to-edit
  // (`editingEnabled` below) has none of the lane's geometry requirements,
  // so it stays armed on every axis/mode/groupMode regardless of `lane`.
  const lane = laneAvailability(axis, mode, groupMode, slots);
  // F-1: `editing` ARMS click-to-edit — see `PortEdgeProps.editing`'s own
  // doc. `editMode` picks WHICH editor a click opens; resolved here rather
  // than at each leaf so every slot renderer downstream can stay ignorant
  // of `laneAvailability` entirely. `"multi"` degrades to `"single"` when
  // the lane genuinely cannot open (see `PortEdgeProps.editMode`'s own doc
  // for why a graceful fallback beats a click that does nothing) — this is
  // the ONE place that decision is made, so a caller who asks for `"multi"`
  // on, say, a row edge gets ordinary single-port editing everywhere
  // instead of each slot separately reinventing the same fallback.
  const editingEnabled = editing;
  const resolvedEditMode: "single" | "multi" = editMode === "multi" && lane.available ? "multi" : "single";
  // F-1: the rail-wide lane's own open/closed state — local, because
  // opening it is now a CONSEQUENCE of a port click (`onRequestLaneOpen`
  // below), not a direct reflection of `editing` the way it was before this
  // redesign (that was the bug: arming and showing were the same bit).
  const [laneOpenLocal, setLaneOpenLocal] = useState(false);
  // P-056: `laneOpen` supplied => the HOST owns the open/closed bit, and the
  // uncontrolled state below is simply unused. Both paths converge on ONE
  // `laneOpen` from here down, so every reader (the card-press guard, the
  // overlay, the sortable's own exclusivity) is unchanged and cannot drift.
  const laneControlled = laneOpenProp !== undefined;
  const laneOpen = (laneControlled ? laneOpenProp : laneOpenLocal) && lane.available;
  const setLaneOpen = (next: boolean) => {
    if (laneControlled) onLaneOpenChange?.(next);
    else setLaneOpenLocal(next);
  };
  // Mirrors the old D12/A12 safety net, now scoped to `laneOpen` instead of
  // `editing` itself: if the rail stops being armed, or the lane's own
  // preconditions lapse mid-edit (a live groupMode/mode change), or the
  // caller drops back to `"single"`, close the lane rather than leave it
  // mounted and mismatched. Content-lossless for the same reason as before
  // — every keystroke already wrote through `onItemsChange`.
  //
  // P-056: scoped to the UNCONTROLLED lane. A controlled lane is not armed by
  // `editing` in the first place — that is the whole point of the seam — so
  // reading arming here would close the host's lane on the frame it opened.
  // The precondition half of the old guard did not move: it is now the
  // `&& lane.available` on `laneOpen` itself, which both paths go through.
  useEffect(() => {
    if (laneOpenLocal && (!editingEnabled || resolvedEditMode !== "multi")) setLaneOpenLocal(false);
  }, [laneOpenLocal, editingEnabled, resolvedEditMode]);
  const handlePortLabelWrite = (id: string, value: string) => {
    onItemsChange?.(items.map((it) => (it.id === id ? { ...it, label: value } : it)));
  };
  // `reorderChord`: the keyboard move, and the focus hand-back it owes once
  // the host has re-rendered this edge in the new order (see
  // `captureEditingFocus` for why focus needs handing back at all). The
  // restore runs in the layout effect of the render that carries the new
  // `items` — before paint, and before CodeMirror's deferred blur-commit
  // (`codeEditor.tsx`'s `setTimeout(0)`) could decide the editor was left.
  const pendingFocusRestore = useRef<(() => void) | null>(null);
  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    const restore = pendingFocusRestore.current;
    pendingFocusRestore.current = null;
    restore?.();
  });
  const handleReorderKey =
    reorderChord && mode === "auto"
      ? (id: string, event: KeyboardEvent) => {
          const delta = reorderDeltaForKey(event, axis, reorderChord);
          if (delta === null) return false;
          const next = moveItemOneSlot(items, allSlots, slots.length, id, delta);
          if (next !== items) {
            pendingFocusRestore.current = captureEditingFocus(event.target);
            onItemsChange?.(next);
          }
          // Claimed even when nothing moved (an end, a grouped member): the
          // chord means "move this port" on this axis, so it never quietly
          // turns back into a caret move at the ends of the rail.
          return true;
        }
      : undefined;
  const reorderHint = handleReorderKey && reorderChord ? reorderHintText(axis, reorderChord) : undefined;
  const resolvedTextLayout = textLayout ?? inwardTextLayout(edge);
  // The lane's own line order: the VISUAL (dot) order, not necessarily
  // `items`' raw array order — a grouped family's members render
  // contiguously beside each other's dots (GroupSlotBody), and the lane
  // must match what's actually on screen, one line per dot top to bottom.
  const visualOrder = slots.flatMap((s) => s.members.map((m) => itemsById.get(m.id)).filter((it): it is PortEdgeItem => it != null));

  // B5/B7: every slot — single or grouped — shares this ONE fixed main-axis
  // size and this ONE inter-slot gap, computed the SAME way `interSlotGapPx`
  // assumes (see its own doc). `mode==="custom"` has no pitch guarantee to
  // begin with (author positions), so it keeps the DEFAULT_ALIGN_GAP fallback
  // rather than this edge's real distribution math.
  //
  // WHY the gap's "count" is the PORT count of the RENDERED slots, not
  // `slots.length` (every rendered DOM box): under a `distribute` scheme,
  // grouping merges N ports into 1 box, so `slots.length` SHRINKS — feeding
  // that shrunk count into the space-between/evenly/around division changes
  // how much free space each remaining gap absorbs, which would make a
  // grouped edge's pitch drift from its OWN ungrouped pitch (A5's actual
  // requirement is "equals the ungrouped pitch for the same items", not
  // merely "uniform"). The total content size (`portCount * slotMainPx`) and
  // the total number of gaps a fully-ungrouped edge would have
  // (`portCount - 1`) are both invariant to how those ports happen to be
  // visually clustered — solving the self-consistency equation (internal
  // group gap == the gap flexbox computes BETWEEN the fewer top-level boxes
  // it actually sees) confirms this is the fixed point: using the PORT count
  // here makes the internal group gap and the real inter-box gap land on the
  // exact same number, by construction, not by coincidence.
  //
  // `maxVisible` (follow-up feedback item 2/4): the count is scoped to the
  // VISIBLE slots' own members, not `items.length` — a cut edge's pitch is
  // computed for what's actually on the track, the same way it would be if
  // the caller had simply passed fewer `items` to begin with.
  // Fixed regardless of `showCard` — see EMPTY_SLOT_MIN_PX's own comment;
  // the card overlay draws inside this footprint, it never grows it.
  const slotMainPx = EMPTY_SLOT_MIN_PX;
  const contentMainPx = (axis === "row" ? resolvedSize.width : resolvedSize.height) - 2 * CONTAINER_BORDER - 2 * CONTAINER_PADDING;
  const visiblePortCount = slots.reduce((n, s) => n + s.members.length, 0);
  const autoGapPx = interSlotGapPx(distribution, contentMainPx, visiblePortCount, slotMainPx);
  const gapPx = mode === "auto" ? autoGapPx : DEFAULT_ALIGN_GAP;

  // Custom spacing (Zach, 2026-09-22 — see `PortEdgeProps.mode`). Each
  // slot's own main-axis length, in the gap it actually renders with: a
  // grouped slot lays its members out at `gap`, a collapsed family is one
  // slot box.
  const slotLength = (slot: Slot, gap: number) =>
    groupMode === "collapsed" || slot.members.length <= 1 ? slotMainPx : slot.members.length * slotMainPx + (slot.members.length - 1) * gap;
  const slotIds = slots.map((s) => s.slotId);
  const spacing: PortSpacingOptions = {
    order: slotIds,
    minGap: minPortGap,
    span: contentMainPx,
    slotSize: slotMainPx,
    sizes: Object.fromEntries(slots.map((s) => [s.slotId, slotLength(s, gapPx)])),
    mode: customNeighbour,
  };
  // Rule 4: whether THIS render is the one that entered Custom. The ref is
  // the mode the previous commit rendered with — seeded with the first
  // render's own mode, so an edge MOUNTED in Custom counts as already in it
  // and re-validates its stored layout rather than discarding it.
  const renderedModeRef = useRef(mode);
  const entering = mode === "custom" && renderedModeRef.current !== "custom";
  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    renderedModeRef.current = mode;
  });
  const customOffsets =
    mode === "custom"
      ? resolveCustomOffsets(
          freeOffsets,
          autoSlotOffsets(slotIds, Object.fromEntries(slots.map((s) => [s.slotId, slotLength(s, autoGapPx)])), contentMainPx, distribution),
          spacing,
          entering,
        )
      : EMPTY_OFFSETS;
  // The drag in flight on THIS edge: which slot, and how far the pointer has
  // travelled along the edge's own axis (the cross axis is ignored — a port
  // moves along its wall and nowhere else).
  const [customDrag, setCustomDrag] = useState<{ id: string; delta: number } | null>(null);
  const ownsCustomDrag = (id: unknown) => mode === "custom" && slotIds.includes(String(id));
  const mainDelta = (delta: { x: number; y: number }) => (axis === "row" ? delta.x : delta.y);
  const liveCustom = (id: string, delta: number) =>
    constrainPortPositions({ ...customOffsets, [id]: (customOffsets[id] ?? 0) + delta }, { ...spacing, active: id });
  const drag = customDrag && slotIds.includes(customDrag.id) ? liveCustom(customDrag.id, customDrag.delta) : null;
  const shownOffsets = drag ? drag.offsets : customOffsets;
  // The min-gap marker's pairs: only the run of tight pairs the DRAGGED port
  // belongs to (itself against the neighbour it stopped at, or every port it
  // is pushing). A pair the author left tight elsewhere on the wall is not
  // what this gesture is running into, so it gets no marker.
  const heldPairs: [string, string][] = [];
  if (drag && customDrag) {
    const tight = new Set(drag.tightPairs.map(([from, to]) => `${from}|${to}`));
    const at = slotIds.indexOf(customDrag.id);
    for (let i = at; i > 0 && tight.has(`${slotIds[i - 1]}|${slotIds[i]}`); i--) heldPairs.push([slotIds[i - 1], slotIds[i]]);
    for (let i = at; i < slotIds.length - 1 && tight.has(`${slotIds[i]}|${slotIds[i + 1]}`); i++) heldPairs.push([slotIds[i], slotIds[i + 1]]);
  }
  // WHY the edge commits its own Custom drop rather than leaving it to
  // whichever `DndContext` owns the drag: the drop position is the
  // CONSTRAINED one, and only this edge knows its order, span and gap — a
  // provider's `onDragEnd` would see a raw pointer delta. One monitor per
  // edge, filtered to its own slot ids, works under a standalone context
  // and a shared `PortEdgeDndProvider` alike.
  useDndMonitor({
    onDragStart: ({ active }) => {
      if (ownsCustomDrag(active.id)) setCustomDrag({ id: String(active.id), delta: 0 });
    },
    onDragMove: ({ active, delta }) => {
      if (ownsCustomDrag(active.id)) setCustomDrag({ id: String(active.id), delta: mainDelta(delta) });
    },
    onDragEnd: ({ active, delta }) => {
      if (!ownsCustomDrag(active.id)) return;
      setCustomDrag(null);
      onFreeOffsetsChange?.(liveCustom(String(active.id), mainDelta(delta)).offsets);
    },
    onDragCancel: () => setCustomDrag(null),
  });
  // Rules 4/5: whatever the edge resolved on its own (the Auto snapshot on
  // entering Custom, a re-validation after an add/remove/reorder) is written
  // back, so the host's stored layout is always the one on screen — a
  // round trip through Auto then has something true to restore.
  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    if (mode !== "custom" || customDrag || !onFreeOffsetsChange) return;
    const keys = Object.keys(customOffsets);
    const same = keys.length === Object.keys(freeOffsets).length && keys.every((id) => freeOffsets[id] === customOffsets[id]);
    if (!same) onFreeOffsetsChange(customOffsets);
  });

  // W1 (docs/WORK-ORDER-2026-09-18.md) — anchor-space port layout. Auto
  // mode's cards no longer get their position from flex plus a sortable
  // strategy: a registered `PortLayoutFn` (portEdge.layout.ts) computes
  // where every slot sits, looked up by the SAME names `SPACING_SCHEMES`/
  // `AlignConfig` already use — the 3 SpacingScheme distributions and
  // `aligned` are registry entries wrapping `mainAxisPositions`, not a
  // second, parallel implementation (see that file's own doc).
  //
  // `anchored` (`AnchoredConfig`, ported from notebook-lab `27b4314`) is
  // one more registry entry, keyed by its own mode name and handed the
  // edge's own config — NOT a branch that lays it out as `aligned`. Before
  // this port the fallback below mapped every non-`distribute` mode to
  // `"aligned"`, so an anchored edge would have silently packed from the
  // start (W1h's handoff note named exactly that trap).
  const layoutName = anchorLayout?.name ?? (distribution.mode === "distribute" ? distribution.scheme : distribution.mode);
  const layoutFn = portLayout(layoutName);
  const layoutArgs = anchorLayout ? anchorLayout.args : distribution.mode === "distribute" ? undefined : distribution;
  /** An `anchored` edge's cards sit where the HOST's numbers put them, so
   *  they carry no drag (`AnchorSlotCard`'s `fixed`) — see `AnchoredConfig`
   *  for why a host that derives positions from its own data wants the
   *  geometry without the gesture. An `anchorLayout` override still wins
   *  the geometry (it did before this mode existed) and stays draggable. */
  const fixedCards = !anchorLayout && distribution.mode === "anchored";

  // W1: "the drag preview is the real thing" (the work order's own
  // words) — while a drag is live over this rail, anchors are computed
  // for the TENTATIVE reordered slot array (`arrayMove` at the live
  // `over` index) rather than an animation approximating it, so every
  // non-dragged card visibly sits where it would actually land, through
  // the SAME `PortLayoutFn` that renders the resting state.
  // `useDndMonitor`, not a prop: the owning `DndContext` belongs to
  // `PortEdgeDndProvider` or `StandalonePortEdge`, either of which may
  // sit several components above this one, and `useDndMonitor` is
  // `@dnd-kit/core`'s own supported way for any descendant to observe a
  // session it doesn't itself own. (Custom mode's own monitor, above, is
  // separate on purpose: it tracks a constrained DELTA, this tracks an
  // ORDER, and `visualSlots` ignores a session whenever `mode` is custom.)
  const [dragSession, setDragSession] = useState<{ activeId: string; overId: string | null } | null>(null);
  useDndMonitor({
    onDragStart: ({ active }) => setDragSession({ activeId: String(active.id), overId: null }),
    onDragOver: ({ active, over }) => setDragSession({ activeId: String(active.id), overId: over ? String(over.id) : null }),
    onDragEnd: () => setDragSession(null),
    onDragCancel: () => setDragSession(null),
  });
  const visualSlots = useMemo(() => {
    if (mode !== "auto" || !dragSession?.overId || dragSession.activeId === dragSession.overId) return slots;
    const oldIndex = slots.findIndex((s) => s.slotId === dragSession.activeId);
    const newIndex = slots.findIndex((s) => s.slotId === dragSession.overId);
    if (oldIndex === -1 || newIndex === -1) return slots;
    return arrayMove(slots, oldIndex, newIndex);
  }, [slots, mode, dragSession]);

  // W1: the cross-axis position a linear (built-in) `PortLayoutFn` can't
  // compute itself — it only ever learns this rail's OWN axis length
  // (`rail.mainSize`), never the perpendicular one (`Anchor`'s own doc,
  // portEdge.layout.ts, explains why). This is the exact number
  // flexbox's `align-items: center` used to produce for free; centring
  // it here once is what keeps the built-ins' RESTING position
  // byte-identical to before this component stopped laying auto-mode
  // cards out with flex. `anchorLayout` (a genuinely non-linear
  // registrant) never needs this — its own `Anchor` already carries a
  // real point on BOTH axes, which is the whole reason it's a separate
  // branch below rather than one more `axis`-keyed case.
  const crossContentPx = (axis === "row" ? resolvedSize.height : resolvedSize.width) - 2 * CONTAINER_BORDER - 2 * CONTAINER_PADDING;
  const crossCenter = crossContentPx / 2;
  // Every entry here is the CARD'S CENTRE, in the track's content-box
  // coordinate frame (the same frame `contentMainPx`/`crossContentPx`
  // already use) — `AnchorSlotCard`'s renderer, below, subtracts half a
  // card's footprint uniformly, the one formula every registrant's
  // output (linear or not) goes through on its way to `left`/`top`.
  const anchorBySlotId = new Map<string, Anchor>();
  // Per-slot MAIN-AXIS span, for the one case a card ISN'T the uniform
  // `slotMainPx` square: a real (not collapsed) group. Absent for every
  // other slot — the render site below falls back to `slotMainPx`, same
  // as the cross axis always does (a group only ever widens along its
  // OWN edge's axis; `GroupSlotBody` never grows the cross dimension).
  const mainSpanBySlotId = new Map<string, number>();
  if (mode === "auto" && anchorLayout) {
    // The escape hatch: a registered layout owns BOTH coordinates
    // itself — no cross-axis override, since a non-linear layout's
    // entire point is that "cross" varies too. Its rail still only
    // states ONE axis length (`rail.mainSize`, `axis`) for a caller that
    // wants to build an ordinary linear layout against it; a registrant
    // that ignores `axis` (an ellipse, ordered by ANGLE, not aisle) is
    // free to.
    const rail: PortLayoutRail = { mainSize: contentMainPx, cardMain: slotMainPx, axis, gap: gapPx };
    if (layoutFn) {
      const pseudoItems: PortEdgeItem[] = visualSlots.map((s) => ({ id: s.slotId, label: "" }));
      const anchors = layoutFn(pseudoItems, rail, layoutArgs);
      visualSlots.forEach((s, i) => {
        const a = anchors[i];
        if (a) anchorBySlotId.set(s.slotId, { ...a, side: a.side ?? edge });
      });
    }
  } else if (mode === "auto") {
    // W1: the registry's `PortLayoutFn` only ever learns ONE uniform
    // `cardMain` — true for every slot except a REAL (not collapsed)
    // multi-member group, whose on-screen span is wider
    // (`GroupSlotBody`'s own flex row of members). `PortEdgeItem` has no
    // member count for a `PortLayoutFn` to read one from, so a real
    // group is routed around the registry entirely, through
    // `packVariableSlotSpans` — see that function's own doc.
    const hasRealGroup = groupMode === "grouped" && visualSlots.some((s) => s.members.length > 1);
    // Leading-edge position + this slot's own main-axis span (see
    // `slotMainSpan`), one per visible slot — `axisAnchor`'s own doc
    // explains why a `PortLayoutFn` reports the leading edge rather than
    // a centre; this is where that gets turned into one.
    let leading: number[];
    let spans: number[];
    if (hasRealGroup) {
      spans = visualSlots.map((s) => slotMainSpan(s.members.length, slotMainPx, gapPx));
      visualSlots.forEach((s, i) => mainSpanBySlotId.set(s.slotId, spans[i]));
    } else {
      spans = visualSlots.map(() => slotMainPx);
    }
    if (hasRealGroup && distribution.mode !== "anchored") {
      leading = packVariableSlotSpans(spans, distribution, gapPx, contentMainPx);
    } else {
      // A real group on an `anchored` edge still comes through the
      // registry: the `"anchored"` entry never reads `rail.cardMain` (the
      // host's offset IS the slot's leading edge, whatever its span), so
      // the uniform-`cardMain` limit above does not apply to it.
      const rail: PortLayoutRail = { mainSize: contentMainPx, cardMain: slotMainPx, axis, gap: gapPx };
      // A `PortLayoutFn` only ever needs identity + count/order — a real
      // `PortEdgeItem` for every rendered SLOT (not raw `items`; a
      // singleton or collapsed slot is the actual positionable unit,
      // same as the old `useSortable({id: slot.slotId})` registration
      // it replaces).
      const pseudoItems: PortEdgeItem[] = visualSlots.map((s) => ({ id: s.slotId, label: "" }));
      leading = layoutFn ? layoutFn(pseudoItems, rail, layoutArgs).map((a) => (axis === "row" ? a.x : a.y)) : visualSlots.map(() => 0);
    }
    visualSlots.forEach((s, i) => {
      const mainCentre = (leading[i] ?? 0) + spans[i] / 2;
      const anchor: Anchor = axis === "row" ? { x: mainCentre, y: crossCenter, side: edge } : { x: crossCenter, y: mainCentre, side: edge };
      anchorBySlotId.set(s.slotId, anchor);
    });
  }

  const { setNodeRef, isOver } = useDroppable({ id: containerId });
  const trackRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement | null>(null);
  // WHY memoized (not an inline arrow in the JSX below): an inline
  // `(el) => {...}` is a NEW function every render, and React re-runs BOTH
  // halves of a changed ref callback (detach the old one with `null`, then
  // attach the new one) on every such render. React commits ref
  // attachment together with layout effects, child-fibers-before-parent —
  // so `PortLaneOverlay`'s OWN `useLayoutEffect` (a child) could observe
  // `trackRef.current` mid-detach (`null`) on the very render where the
  // lane first mounts, one commit before the parent's ref finished
  // reattaching. Measured live: `trackRef.current` read `true` during
  // `PortLaneOverlay`'s RENDER (a stale value from the previous commit)
  // and `false` inside its layout effect moments later, on the same click.
  // A stable callback identity (`useCallback`) is reused, not
  // detached/reattached, so it never races the child's own effect.
  const setTrackRef = useCallback(
    (el: HTMLDivElement | null) => {
      setNodeRef(el);
      trackRef.current = el;
    },
    [setNodeRef],
  );

  // See `useMoreIndicatorPosition`'s own doc for why this is called HERE
  // (inside PortEdgeBody, the component that owns `trackRef`) rather than
  // inside a separate child component.
  const moreIndicatorPos = useMoreIndicatorPosition(trackRef, axis, hiddenPortCount);

  const trackStyle: CSSProperties = {
    // The isolation invariant (paintOrder.ts): this root emits the
    // `memberContent`/`dragActive` (cards) and `laneOverlay` tiers, so
    // it must wall them off from anything outside it — same reasoning
    // as Block's own root and BlockPort's wrapper.
    ...ISOLATED_ROOT_STYLE,
    boxSizing: "border-box",
    width: resolvedSize.width,
    height: resolvedSize.height,
    position: "relative",
    display: "flex",
    flexDirection: axis === "row" ? "row" : "column",
    alignItems: "center",
    padding: CONTAINER_PADDING,
    // The skin seam — `showContainer`'s dashed dev outline reads
    // `--bbox-skin-stroke` (the same token Block's own border reads) with
    // today's `var(--border)` as its fallback; `radius: 6` -> the token,
    // same fallback literal. The `showContainer=false` branch stays
    // `solid transparent` untouched — a transparent border needs no skin,
    // it is already invisible in every one.
    border: `${CONTAINER_BORDER}px ${showContainer ? "dashed var(--bbox-skin-stroke, var(--border))" : "solid transparent"}`,
    borderRadius: "var(--bbox-skin-radius, 6px)",
    background: isOver ? "color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 14%, transparent)" : undefined,
    ...(mode === "custom" ? {} : distributionStyle(axis, distribution)),
  };

  return (
    <div
      ref={setTrackRef}
      data-slot="port-edge"
      data-edge={edge}
      data-unknown-groups={unknownIds.length > 0 ? unknownIds.join(",") : undefined}
      style={trackStyle}
      // D13, restored: starting a drag CLOSES the lane rather than
      // disabling the sortable — `useSortable({disabled:true})` still
      // spreads `attributes` (role/tabIndex) and never reaches Custom
      // mode's `useDraggable` at all, so it doesn't actually stop a drag;
      // a capture-phase pointerdown on any card does, unconditionally,
      // and makes the exclusivity legible instead of emergent (see the
      // codeEditor's OWN capture listener, which this mirrors). Gated on
      // `laneOpen` alone now, not `editing` — a per-port single-line edit
      // has nothing shared to protect, only the rail-wide document does.
      onPointerDownCapture={
        laneOpen
          ? (event) => {
              if (event.target instanceof Element && event.target.closest('[data-slot="port-edge-card"]')) {
                setLaneOpen(false);
              }
            }
          : undefined
      }
    >
      {showCenterLine ? <EdgeLineMark axis={axis} /> : null}
      {laneOpen && (
        <PortLaneOverlay
          trackRef={trackRef}
          visualOrder={visualOrder}
          onItemsChange={onItemsChange}
          onClose={() => setLaneOpen(false)}
          language={laneLanguage}
          dividerAfter={dividerAfter}
          onDividerAfterChange={onDividerAfterChange}
        />
      )}
      {hiddenPortCount > 0 && moreIndicatorPos && (
        // RULING 7's seam, in one element. The POSITION is this
        // component's (it owns `trackRef`, and the anchor is measured off
        // the last visible port's real box — `useMoreIndicatorPosition`);
        // WHAT gets drawn at that position is the caller's, which is how
        // `editor/portOverflowChip.tsx` supplies chrome that `components/`
        // is not allowed to import. `pointerEvents` is `auto` only when a
        // caller actually rendered something interactive — the bare
        // fallback below is text, and text that eats clicks over a track
        // would be a new way to miss a port.
        <span
          data-slot="port-edge-more"
          style={{
            position: "absolute",
            left: moreIndicatorPos.left,
            top: moreIndicatorPos.top,
            transform: axis === "row" ? "translateY(-50%)" : undefined,
            whiteSpace: "nowrap",
            // WHY `flex` rather than the default block: as a block, this
            // box's height is a LINE box, so half-leading pads whatever
            // sits in it and the thing a reader actually sees ends up a
            // couple of pixels below the anchor the hook measured — and
            // on a row edge, `translateY(-50%)` then centres the leading
            // instead of the content. A flex container blockifies its
            // child, so the box is exactly the marker's own height and
            // the measured anchor is the marker's real position. Measured
            // at 2.125px of drift before this line.
            display: "flex",
            alignItems: "center",
            // Supplied chrome can open something (the chip's own list of
            // hidden ports), so it rides the same tier the lane overlay
            // does — above every slot card on the track, walled off by
            // this root's own `ISOLATED_ROOT_STYLE`. The bare fallback is
            // inert text and claims no tier at all.
            ...(renderOverflow
              ? { pointerEvents: "auto", zIndex: paintTier("laneOverlay") }
              : { fontSize: 12, fontStyle: "italic", color: "var(--muted-foreground)", pointerEvents: "none" }),
          }}
        >
          {renderOverflow ? renderOverflow({ hiddenCount: hiddenPortCount, hiddenItems, edge, axis }) : `+${hiddenPortCount} more`}
        </span>
      )}
      {drag &&
        heldPairs.map(([from, to]) => (
          <MinGapMarker
            key={`${from}|${to}`}
            edge={edge}
            axis={axis}
            from={drag.offsets[from] + spacing.sizes![from] - slotMainPx / 2}
            to={drag.offsets[to] + slotMainPx / 2}
            crossPx={axis === "column" ? resolvedSize.width : resolvedSize.height}
          />
        ))}
      {mode === "custom"
        ? slots.map((slot) => (
            <FreeSlotCard
              key={slot.slotId}
              slot={slot}
              axis={axis}
              edge={edge}
              itemsById={itemsById}
              showPorts={showPorts}
              showCard={showCard}
              collapsed={groupMode === "collapsed"}
              slotMainPx={slotMainPx}
              gapPx={gapPx}
              color={colorMap.get(slot.slotId)}
              x={(drag && customDrag?.id === slot.slotId ? customOffsets : shownOffsets)[slot.slotId] ?? 0}
              shift={drag && customDrag?.id === slot.slotId ? shownOffsets[slot.slotId] - customOffsets[slot.slotId] : 0}
              renderItem={renderItem}
              renderCollapsed={renderCollapsed}
              textLayoutOverride={resolvedTextLayout}
              editingEnabled={editingEnabled}
              onLabelWrite={handlePortLabelWrite}
              labelLanguage={laneLanguage}
              variant={variant}
              editMode={resolvedEditMode}
              onRequestLaneOpen={() => setLaneOpen(true)}
            />
          ))
        : slots.map((slot) => {
            // W1: every anchor above is a CARD CENTRE (built-in or
            // `anchorLayout`, uniform or grouped) — `CONTAINER_PADDING`
            // is `FreeSlotCard`'s own compensation for position:absolute
            // ignoring the track's own CSS padding (that file's doc),
            // and subtracting half the card's own footprint turns a
            // centre into the top-left `left`/`top` a box needs. Only
            // the MAIN axis footprint ever differs from `slotMainPx`
            // (a real group, wider along its OWN edge's axis —
            // `mainSpanBySlotId`); the cross footprint is always the
            // uniform square, on every registrant, linear or not.
            const anchor = anchorBySlotId.get(slot.slotId) ?? { x: crossCenter, y: crossCenter };
            const mainSpan = mainSpanBySlotId.get(slot.slotId) ?? slotMainPx;
            const left = CONTAINER_PADDING + anchor.x - (axis === "row" ? mainSpan : slotMainPx) / 2;
            const top = CONTAINER_PADDING + anchor.y - (axis === "row" ? slotMainPx : mainSpan) / 2;
            return (
              <AnchorSlotCard
                key={slot.slotId}
                left={left}
                top={top}
                fixed={fixedCards}
                slot={slot}
                axis={axis}
                edge={edge}
                itemsById={itemsById}
                showPorts={showPorts}
                showCard={showCard}
                collapsed={groupMode === "collapsed"}
                slotMainPx={slotMainPx}
                gapPx={gapPx}
                color={colorMap.get(slot.slotId)}
                renderItem={renderItem}
                renderCollapsed={renderCollapsed}
                textLayoutOverride={resolvedTextLayout}
                editingEnabled={editingEnabled}
                onLabelWrite={handlePortLabelWrite}
                labelLanguage={laneLanguage}
                variant={variant}
                editMode={resolvedEditMode}
                onRequestLaneOpen={() => setLaneOpen(true)}
                onReorderKey={handleReorderKey}
                reorderHint={reorderHint}
              />
            );
          })}
    </div>
  );
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* PortEdge — one edge of a Block: a droppable, sortable strip of       */
/* ports. Mounts its own DndContext only when not already inside a      */
/* `PortEdgeDndProvider` (see this file's own module doc, D6).          */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

export function PortEdge(props: PortEdgeProps) {
  const inScope = useContext(PortEdgeDndScope);
  if (inScope) return <PortEdgeBody {...props} />;
  return <StandalonePortEdge {...props} />;
}

/** The standalone wrapper: owns its own `DndContext`, and its own
 *  `handleDragEnd` for the ONE thing a lone edge needs to do — reorder its
 *  own slots (Auto). Custom's drop is `PortEdgeBody`'s, under either
 *  context. A nested `PortEdge` never runs this; the surrounding
 *  `PortEdgeDndProvider`'s own `onDragEnd` does the equivalent work across
 *  every edge it owns. */
function StandalonePortEdge(props: PortEdgeProps) {
  const { items, onItemsChange, mode = "auto", groups = "", groupMode = "off" } = props;
  const sensors = useSensors(useSensor(PointerSensor, { activationConstraint: { distance: 4 } }));

  function handleDragEnd(event: DragEndEvent) {
    const { active, over } = event;
    const slotId = String(active.id);
    // Custom's drop is committed by `PortEdgeBody` itself, constrained —
    // see its `useDndMonitor`.
    if (mode === "custom") return;
    if (!over || over.id === active.id) return;
    const itemIds = items.map((i) => i.id);
    const { set: groupingSet } = groupingSetFromText(groups, itemIds);
    const slots = computeSlots(itemIds, groupingSet, groupMode);
    const next = reorderItemsBySlot(items, slots, slotId, String(over.id));
    onItemsChange?.(next);
  }

  return (
    <DndContext sensors={sensors} collisionDetection={nearestAnchor} onDragEnd={handleDragEnd}>
      <PortEdgeBody {...props} />
    </DndContext>
  );
}

// src/components/portLane.ts — the multi-line port editor's identity
// reconcile: an LCS-based diff between the lane's before/after line lists
// that decides which port keeps its id across an edit. Adapted from
// SystemSketch's shipped default port editor
// (systemsketch/src/blocks/portLane.ts:102-176, imported at
// BlockInlineEditor.tsx:15 — NOT the other `reconcilePortLane` at
// blocks/babble/portTextShared.ts:186, a babble prototype with a different,
// exact-name-then-ordinal contract and zero importers outside babble/) —
// stripped of the seven concerns this app has no counterpart for:
// BlockShapeProps, header/effect ports, canonicalizeInputPortLinks,
// normalizeBlockPortRows, reconcileEffectPorts, portInHeader/isEffectPort,
// withBlockPortSection, and parsePortSignature/formatPortSignature's
// in_N/out_N id scheme + grammar (docs/PORT-EDGE-PLAN-PHASE4.md §1, D10).
//
// Contract, precisely (phase-4 doc §2 — every row there is this function's
// spec, one edit -> one result):
//   1. LCS over the two line lists, BYTE-IDENTICAL only — a line that
//      survives the diff keeps the SAME port object, by reference.
//   2. The leftover (unmatched) lines on each side are zipped POSITIONALLY
//      — by RANK among leftovers, never by content — which is what keeps
//      an in-place rename's id: the common case, not a special one.
//   3. Anything still unmatched mints a fresh port via the caller's own
//      `mintItem` (D9: id minting is host-owned and ids are opaque — this
//      module never invents one).
//
// D10: `label` IS the raw line — no grammar, no re-parse, no
// canonicalisation. What makes the round trip (`formatPortLane` -> edit ->
// `reconcilePortLane`) exact is therefore not the absence of a grammar but
// the presence of ONE: the line codec below, whose encode is injective and
// whose decode is its exact left inverse. That property is the whole reason
// the no-op case is referentially idempotent (see the purity note on
// `reconcilePortLane` itself) — an earlier version of this header claimed
// "exact by construction" while the codec was not invertible, and it was
// silently rewriting labels (judge finding 1, 2026-09-22).
import type { PortEdgeItem } from "./portEdge";

/**
 * `commonLines` — a standard LCS table (suffix-built, `table[i][j]` = the
 * length of the longest common subsequence of `a[i..]`/`b[j..]`) plus a
 * forward optimal backtrack. The tie-break (`table[i+1][j] >= table[i][j+1]`
 * — prefer advancing `a` over `b` on a tie) is what makes a two-line swap
 * resolve the way a human expects, matching the donor exactly
 * (portLane.ts:62-86).
 *
 * PRIOR ART, named because "LCS" deserves its actual citation. This is the
 * textbook QUADRATIC-time, QUADRATIC-space dynamic program — not one of the
 * refinements — and the cleanest statement of exactly that baseline is the
 * opening of D. S. Hirschberg, "A linear space algorithm for computing
 * maximal common subsequences", Communications of the ACM 18(6):341-343,
 * 1975: "The problem of finding a longest common subsequence of two strings
 * has been solved in quadratic time and space." That sentence describes THIS
 * function; the paper then improves the space, which we deliberately do not
 * need (a port lane is tens of lines, not a genome). Its ancestor recurrence
 * is R. A. Wagner and M. J. Fischer, "The String-to-String Correction
 * Problem", Journal of the ACM 21(1):168-173, 1974.
 *
 * Both citations verified 2026-09-17 against dl.acm.org's own records
 * (doi 10.1145/360825.360861 and 10.1145/321796.321811) — venue, volume,
 * pages and year all confirmed there. The PAPERS' full texts were not read;
 * nothing here depends on them beyond the baseline they name, and the
 * implementation came from the donor, not from either paper.
 *
 * NOT prior art, and not claimed as any: the leftover-zipping in
 * `reconcilePortLane` below (step 2 — pair unmatched lines POSITIONALLY by
 * rank). That is this repo's own product rule for "a rename keeps its id",
 * invented here, with no algorithm behind it.
 */
function lcsTable(a: string[], b: string[]): number[][] {
  const n = a.length;
  const m = b.length;
  const table: number[][] = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array<number>(m + 1).fill(0));
  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    for (let j = m - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
      table[i][j] = a[i] === b[j] ? table[i + 1][j + 1] + 1 : Math.max(table[i + 1][j], table[i][j + 1]);
    }
  }
  return table;
}

function backtrackMatches(a: string[], b: string[], table: number[][]): { ai: number; bi: number }[] {
  const matches: { ai: number; bi: number }[] = [];
  let i = 0;
  let j = 0;
  while (i < a.length && j < b.length) {
    if (a[i] === b[j]) {
      matches.push({ ai: i, bi: j });
      i++;
      j++;
    } else if (table[i + 1][j] >= table[i][j + 1]) {
      i++;
    } else {
      j++;
    }
  }
  return matches;
}

/**
 * The one-line-per-port ENCODE/DECODE pair — a codec, and the only reason
 * `formatPortLane` -> edit -> `reconcilePortLane` can be the IDENTITY on an
 * unedited document. Every caller that builds or reads lane TEXT shares it:
 * this file's own `formatPortLane`/`reconcilePortLane` and
 * `returnDividers.ts`'s `formatRailLane`/`reconcileRailLane`.
 *
 * The transport is line-oriented — one port per physical line, joined with
 * `\n` — so a label's own `\n` is IN-BAND and must be escaped, or one item
 * silently becomes two ports on the next open. (An embedded `\n` is
 * unreachable from the keyboard — `singleLineGuard`/multiline both filter it
 * — but reachable from story code, a paste, or a host import.)
 *
 * TWO marks, because ONE cannot work (judge finding 1, 2026-09-22). The
 * first version of this pair replaced `\n` with `␊` on the way out and `␊`
 * with `\n` on the way back: two substitutions that look symmetric and are
 * not inverses. A label that legitimately contained `␊` was emitted
 * UNCHANGED and then decoded to a different string, so opening the lane and
 * touching nothing rewrote the user's port; and `a\n␊b` and `a␊\nb` both
 * encoded to `a␊␊b`, which is the same fault stated as a collision. An
 * escape scheme has to escape its own escape marker. This one does:
 *
 *   `\n` -> `␊`      the newline mark — the glyph a human sees in the lane
 *   `␊`  -> `␛␊`     a LITERAL newline mark, escaped
 *   `␛`  -> `␛␛`     the escape mark itself, escaped
 *
 * so in encoded text a `␛` ALWAYS opens a two-character pair and a bare
 * `␊` ALWAYS means a newline. `decodeLaneLine` is one left-to-right pass
 * over exactly those three productions, which is what makes it an exact
 * LEFT INVERSE: `decodeLaneLine(encodeLaneLine(x)) === x` for every string
 * `x`, and `encodeLaneLine` is therefore injective. Both are proven by a
 * sweep over an alphabet that CONTAINS the two marks, not by examples —
 * `portLane.test.ts`, which is the test the old pair could never have
 * passed.
 *
 * Decode is also TOTAL, because the text it reads is not only encoder
 * output: a human can type `␛` straight into the lane. An unpaired mark
 * therefore decodes to itself rather than throwing.
 *
 * `encodeLaneLine` returns the label byte-identical whenever it contains
 * none of the three characters — which is every real port name — so no
 * stored data and nothing on screen changes for anything anyone has
 * actually typed.
 *
 * NOT prior art, and none claimed: this is the textbook "escape the escape
 * marker" rule (the same shape as a C string escape, or `\\` inside a regex
 * character class), derived here from the requirement rather than ported
 * from any implementation.
 */
export const LANE_NEWLINE_MARK = "␊";
export const LANE_ESCAPE_MARK = "␛";

// Built from the constants above rather than written out a second time as
// literals: the marks ARE the wire format, and a pattern that can drift from
// them is the "coordinated only by prose" failure this repo keeps paying
// for. A module-level /g/ regex is safe with `String.replace`, which resets
// `lastIndex` itself on every call.
const ENCODE_PATTERN = new RegExp(`[\\n${LANE_NEWLINE_MARK}${LANE_ESCAPE_MARK}]`, "g");
const DECODE_PATTERN = new RegExp(`${LANE_ESCAPE_MARK}([\\s\\S])|${LANE_NEWLINE_MARK}`, "g");

export function encodeLaneLine(line: string): string {
  return line.replace(ENCODE_PATTERN, (character) => (character === "\n" ? LANE_NEWLINE_MARK : LANE_ESCAPE_MARK + character));
}

/**
 * The exact left inverse of `encodeLaneLine`.
 *
 * ONE left-to-right pass, and that is load-bearing: two chained `replace`
 * calls (`␛␊` -> `␊`, then `␊` -> `\n`) would re-examine their own output
 * and turn an escaped LITERAL mark into a newline — the original bug wearing
 * a different shape. The alternation puts the escape pair FIRST so a `␛`
 * always consumes the character after it, and an unpaired trailing `␛`
 * matches neither branch and survives as itself.
 *
 * `reconcilePortLane` decodes every SURVIVING line through this before it is
 * compared against a port's own raw label or written back with
 * `withPortLaneLine` — that is the one place lane TEXT becomes port DATA.
 */
export function decodeLaneLine(line: string): string {
  return line.replace(DECODE_PATTERN, (_match, escaped: string | undefined) => (escaped === undefined ? "\n" : escaped));
}

/** The lane's document, in port order — one line per port, in the SAME
 *  order the caller's ports are flattened in (portEdge.tsx flattens
 *  `computeSlots`'s visual order, not necessarily `items`' raw array order,
 *  so a grouped family's members stay contiguous with the dots they sit
 *  beside). */
export function formatPortLane(ports: PortEdgeItem[]): string {
  return ports.map((p) => encodeLaneLine(portLaneLine(p))).join("\n");
}

/**
 * The line this port shows in the lane — its own `laneLine` when it declares
 * one, else its rail `label`.
 *
 * P-056. See `PortEdgeItem.laneLine`'s own doc for why a host may have two
 * different strings for one port; here the only thing that matters is that
 * the lane reads and writes ONE of them consistently, so the round trip
 * (`formatPortLane` -> edit -> `reconcilePortLane`) stays exact.
 */
export function portLaneLine(port: PortEdgeItem): string {
  return port.laneLine ?? port.label;
}

/**
 * Put an edited line back on the field it came from.
 *
 * WHY it writes `laneLine` when the item declared one, rather than always
 * writing `label`: `label` is the rail's own string, and on a host where the
 * two differ the rail's string is a PREFIX the host derives from the stored
 * line. Overwriting it with a whole declaration would paint `gain_y: int` on
 * a rail whose whole design ruling is that it must not — and would leave
 * `laneLine` reporting the stale line on the very next render, so the edit
 * would look like it had been rejected.
 */
function withPortLaneLine(port: PortEdgeItem, line: string): PortEdgeItem {
  return port.laneLine !== undefined ? { ...port, laneLine: line } : { ...port, label: line };
}

/**
 * Reconciles `ports` (in lane order) against the lane's edited `text`,
 * minting a fresh port via `mintItem` for every line that ends up
 * unmatched. Pure: matched ports are reused BY REFERENCE (`===`, not just
 * `deepEqual`), an edited line's port goes through `{...port}` once, and —
 * this is what a live "one call per keystroke" write policy needs — a
 * text that already spells `formatPortLane(ports)` returns the exact SAME
 * `ports` array back, so a host's `onItemsChange` never fires on a
 * genuine no-op.
 */
export function reconcilePortLane(ports: PortEdgeItem[], text: string, mintItem: (label: string) => PortEdgeItem): PortEdgeItem[] {
  // ENCODED on both sides of the diff (finding 1's fix): `text` is lane
  // TEXT — whatever `formatPortLane` last emitted, plus whatever was typed
  // on top of it — so it already carries the `␊` placeholder for any
  // embedded newline. `before` has to be encoded the same way, or a port
  // whose raw label contains a real `\n` never equals its own unedited line
  // and gets torn into a rename + a fresh mint instead of recognised as
  // unchanged.
  const before = ports.map((p) => encodeLaneLine(portLaneLine(p)));
  // `text === ""` short-circuits to zero lines — `"".split("\n")` would
  // otherwise produce `[""]`, one port with an empty label, not an empty
  // lane. Select-all + Delete must empty the edge.
  const after = text === "" ? [] : text.split("\n");

  const table = lcsTable(before, after);
  const matches = backtrackMatches(before, after, table);

  // Tier 1: the LCS matches — same port, by reference.
  const keep = new Map<number, PortEdgeItem>();
  const matchedBefore = new Set<number>();
  const matchedAfter = new Set<number>();
  for (const { ai, bi } of matches) {
    keep.set(bi, ports[ai]);
    matchedBefore.add(ai);
    matchedAfter.add(bi);
  }

  // Tier 2: the leftovers on each side, zipped by RANK — not content. This
  // is the tier that keeps an in-place rename's id (the donor's own
  // comment, portLane.ts:120-122): a renamed line is still "the Nth
  // leftover" on both sides.
  const spareBefore: number[] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < before.length; i++) if (!matchedBefore.has(i)) spareBefore.push(i);
  const spareAfter: number[] = [];
  for (let j = 0; j < after.length; j++) if (!matchedAfter.has(j)) spareAfter.push(j);
  const pairCount = Math.min(spareBefore.length, spareAfter.length);
  for (let k = 0; k < pairCount; k++) {
    keep.set(spareAfter[k], ports[spareBefore[k]]);
  }

  // Tier 3: still unmatched -> a fresh port. One `mintItem` call per new
  // line, in order, so two new lines in one edit mint two distinct ids.
  // DECODED here — the one place a survivor's text is turned back into a
  // real label — so a matched or renamed port's stored `label`/`laneLine`
  // gets its original `\n` back rather than the `␊` placeholder that lived
  // in the TEXT (finding 1's other half: encoding without a matching decode
  // would leave the placeholder symbol itself in the port's data).
  const result = after.map((rawLine, bi) => {
    const label = decodeLaneLine(rawLine);
    const source = keep.get(bi);
    if (!source) return mintItem(label);
    // Only actually EDIT the line when it changed — see the purity note.
    return portLaneLine(source) === label ? source : withPortLaneLine(source, label);
  });

  // Purity: if every resulting port is the SAME object, in the SAME order,
  // as `ports`, return `ports` itself rather than a structurally-equal
  // copy — the no-op case a live write policy hits on every keystroke that
  // doesn't touch THIS particular line.
  if (result.length === ports.length && result.every((p, i) => p === ports[i])) return ports;
  return result;
}

/**
 * src/components/portSpacing.layout.ts — the pure constraint maths behind a
 * `PortEdge`'s Custom spacing mode: where each slot may sit along its wall
 * once the author drags it, and where it starts when the mode is entered.
 *
 * Zach, 2026-09-22: "Prototype toggle between auto and custom spacing on the
 * expanded port edge ... in custom: You just need to respect the ordering of
 * ports — and there is a minimum spacing requirement as well". His sketch
 * draws the Custom wall with two ports close together near the bottom and a
 * red bracket between them labelled "Min space requirement".
 *
 * So Custom is NOT free positioning any more (it was: `freeOffsets` accepted
 * any number >= 0, in any order — portEdge.tsx's `StandalonePortEdge` before
 * this file). Three rules hold for every value this module returns:
 *
 *   1. ORDER — slot `i` never sits past slot `i + 1`. The order is the
 *      `order` array (the edge's `items`), never the numbers themselves.
 *   2. MINIMUM GAP — adjacent PORT centres stay at least `minGap` apart. For
 *      a grouped slot (several ports in one box) that is measured from its
 *      LAST port to the next slot's FIRST port.
 *   3. SPAN — a slot's box stays inside the track's content box
 *      (`[0, span - size]`): below a block's header, above its bottom inset.
 *
 * Units are the SAME as `PortEdgeProps.freeOffsets`: each slot's LEADING
 * edge, in px from the start of the track's content box. No DOM here.
 *
 * Ours, ad hoc — no prior art is claimed. The envelope (`L`/`R` below) is the
 * ordinary earliest/latest-start bound of a chain of minimum separations; it
 * is derived here, not ported.
 */
import { PORT_EDGE_SLOT_MIN_PX } from "./blockPort.layout";
import { clamp, type Distribution } from "./portEdge.layout";

/**
 * The minimum centre-to-centre distance between two adjacent ports in Custom.
 *
 * = `PORT_EDGE_SLOT_MIN_PX` (24, blockPort.layout.ts:83), the fixed main-axis
 * footprint of one port slot — itself the mirror of `portEdge.tsx`'s
 * `EMPTY_SLOT_MIN_PX` (portEdge.tsx:638), pinned equal by `ClassicDag`. At
 * exactly this pitch two slot boxes TOUCH and never overlap, so the dot (12px
 * at `md`), its 40px hit halo's centre and the one-line label all stay inside
 * their own slot. It is the tightest pitch at which the rail's existing
 * geometry still holds — the auto `aligned` rail's pitch (`railPitchPx()`,
 * 24 + 18 = 42) is that footprint plus a comfort gap Custom deliberately
 * lets the author give up.
 */
export const MIN_PORT_GAP_PX = PORT_EDGE_SLOT_MIN_PX;

/** Sub-pixel slack for "is this pair held at the minimum gap". */
const TIGHT_EPSILON_PX = 0.5;

/**
 * What happens when a dragged port meets a neighbour — a taste call, shipped
 * as a switch:
 *   - `"stop"`: the dragged port clamps against its neighbour; nothing else
 *     moves.
 *   - `"push"`: neighbours are shoved along ahead of it, order and gaps
 *     intact, until they reach the end of the span.
 */
export type CustomNeighbourMode = "stop" | "push";

export interface PortSpacingOptions {
  /** Slot ids in track order — the ONLY source of order. */
  order: string[];
  /** Minimum centre-to-centre gap between adjacent ports. */
  minGap: number;
  /** Length of the track's content box along its main axis, px. */
  span: number;
  /** Main-axis size of one single-port slot box. */
  slotSize: number;
  /** A slot's own main-axis size when it is not `slotSize` (a grouped slot).
   *  Missing ids use `slotSize`. */
  sizes?: Record<string, number>;
  mode: CustomNeighbourMode;
  /** The slot being dragged, if any. Without it the call is a plain
   *  validation: `mode` does not matter. */
  active?: string;
}

export interface PortSpacingResult {
  /** Leading-edge offset for every id in `order`, and no other id. */
  offsets: Record<string, number>;
  /**
   * The degenerate case: the span cannot hold every slot at the minimum gap.
   *
   * RULE (decided here, pinned in portSpacing.layout.test.ts): ORDER and the
   * MINIMUM GAP win, the span loses. Slots pack from the span's start at
   * exactly the minimum pitch and run past its far end. Rejected
   * alternative: squeezing them into the span, which is exactly the overlap
   * the minimum gap exists to forbid — a dot hanging past the wall is
   * visible and fixable (grow the block), two dots on one pixel are neither.
   * In this state every slot is pinned, so a drag moves nothing.
   */
  overflow: boolean;
  /** Adjacent pairs held at exactly the minimum gap, in track order — what
   *  the drag-time "min space" marker draws between. */
  tightPairs: [string, string][];
}

function sizeOf(id: string, options: PortSpacingOptions): number {
  return options.sizes?.[id] ?? options.slotSize;
}

/** Minimum distance from slot `i`'s leading edge to slot `i + 1`'s. */
function steps(options: PortSpacingOptions): number[] {
  const { order, minGap, slotSize } = options;
  return order.slice(0, -1).map((id) => sizeOf(id, options) - slotSize + minGap);
}

/**
 * Earliest (`lo`) and latest (`hi`) leading edge each slot can take with
 * every other slot packed as tightly as the rules allow. When the span is too
 * short `hi < lo`, and `clamp` (portEdge.layout.ts) then returns `lo` — which
 * is how the overflow rule above falls out without a special case.
 */
function envelope(options: PortSpacingOptions): { lo: number[]; hi: number[] } {
  const { order, span } = options;
  const n = order.length;
  const step = steps(options);
  const lo = new Array<number>(n).fill(0);
  const hi = new Array<number>(n).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) lo[i] = lo[i - 1] + step[i - 1];
  if (n > 0) hi[n - 1] = span - sizeOf(order[n - 1], options);
  for (let i = n - 2; i >= 0; i--) hi[i] = hi[i + 1] - step[i];
  return { lo, hi };
}

function finite(value: number | undefined): value is number {
  return typeof value === "number" && Number.isFinite(value);
}

/**
 * Clamp every slot into its envelope, then one forward pass lifting each slot
 * to at least one step past its predecessor. Given `positions[i] <= hi[i]`
 * for all i, the lift can never push a slot past its own `hi` (its
 * predecessor is at most `hi[i-1] = hi[i] - step`), so the result satisfies
 * all three rules. Idempotent on an already-valid layout.
 */
function normalize(positions: number[], lo: number[], hi: number[], step: number[]): number[] {
  const out = positions.map((p, i) => clamp(p, lo[i], hi[i]));
  for (let i = 1; i < out.length; i++) out[i] = Math.max(out[i], out[i - 1] + step[i - 1]);
  return out;
}

/**
 * The constraint function. `requested` holds where each slot WANTS to be —
 * typically the current layout with the dragged slot's value replaced by the
 * pointer's. A missing or non-finite id is treated as 0.
 */
export function constrainPortPositions(requested: Record<string, number>, options: PortSpacingOptions): PortSpacingResult {
  const { order, span, active, mode } = options;
  const n = order.length;
  const step = steps(options);
  const { lo, hi } = envelope(options);
  const wanted = order.map((id) => (finite(requested[id]) ? requested[id] : 0));
  const a = active === undefined ? -1 : order.indexOf(active);

  let positions: number[];
  if (a < 0) {
    positions = normalize(wanted, lo, hi, step);
  } else if (mode === "stop") {
    // Neighbours hold still (normalised first, so a stale baseline cannot
    // leave the dragged slot a window that breaks a rule); the dragged slot
    // clamps inside the gap they leave it. Its own request is kept OUT of
    // that normalisation — as `-Infinity` it settles at the earliest slot its
    // predecessor allows, which on a valid baseline moves nobody — or the
    // pointer's value would shove the neighbours it is meant to stop at.
    const baseline = wanted.slice();
    baseline[a] = Number.NEGATIVE_INFINITY;
    const others = normalize(baseline, lo, hi, step);
    const floor = a > 0 ? Math.max(lo[a], others[a - 1] + step[a - 1]) : lo[a];
    const ceiling = a < n - 1 ? Math.min(hi[a], others[a + 1] - step[a]) : hi[a];
    others[a] = clamp(wanted[a], floor, ceiling);
    positions = normalize(others, lo, hi, step);
  } else {
    // Push: the dragged slot may go anywhere its envelope allows; whatever it
    // runs into moves ahead of it, both directions.
    const pushed = wanted.map((p, i) => clamp(p, lo[i], hi[i]));
    for (let i = a + 1; i < n; i++) pushed[i] = Math.max(pushed[i], pushed[i - 1] + step[i - 1]);
    for (let i = a - 1; i >= 0; i--) pushed[i] = Math.min(pushed[i], pushed[i + 1] - step[i]);
    positions = normalize(pushed, lo, hi, step);
  }

  const offsets: Record<string, number> = {};
  order.forEach((id, i) => {
    offsets[id] = positions[i];
  });
  const tightPairs: [string, string][] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
    if (positions[i + 1] - positions[i] - step[i] < TIGHT_EPSILON_PX) tightPairs.push([order[i], order[i + 1]]);
  }
  const overflow = n > 0 && lo[n - 1] > span - sizeOf(order[n - 1], options) + TIGHT_EPSILON_PX;
  return { offsets, overflow, tightPairs };
}

/**
 * Whether a stored Custom layout is still valid AS-IS: exactly one finite
 * offset per slot in `order` (no missing id, no stale one), and every rule
 * already holding — validation would not move anything.
 */
export function customOffsetsValid(stored: Record<string, number>, options: PortSpacingOptions): boolean {
  const keys = Object.keys(stored);
  if (keys.length !== options.order.length) return false;
  if (!options.order.every((id) => finite(stored[id]))) return false;
  const { offsets } = constrainPortPositions(stored, { ...options, active: undefined });
  return options.order.every((id) => Math.abs(offsets[id] - stored[id]) < TIGHT_EPSILON_PX);
}

/**
 * Rule 5 — the edge changed underneath a Custom layout (a port added, removed
 * or reordered). Re-validated rather than reset:
 *   - the heights the author chose survive: the surviving ports' offsets,
 *     sorted, are handed back out in the NEW order, so a reorder makes two
 *     ports trade places instead of one being shoved down the wall;
 *   - a new port starts at its Auto position;
 *   - then the ordinary validation settles order, gap and span.
 */
export function revalidateCustomOffsets(
  stored: Record<string, number>,
  auto: Record<string, number>,
  options: PortSpacingOptions,
): Record<string, number> {
  const present = options.order.filter((id) => finite(stored[id]));
  const heights = present.map((id) => stored[id]).sort((x, y) => x - y);
  const requested: Record<string, number> = {};
  present.forEach((id, i) => {
    requested[id] = heights[i];
  });
  for (const id of options.order) if (!(id in requested)) requested[id] = auto[id] ?? 0;
  return constrainPortPositions(requested, { ...options, active: undefined }).offsets;
}

/**
 * The Custom layout to show.
 *
 * `entering` (rule 4 — Auto -> Custom just happened): a stored layout that is
 * still valid for the current order and count is RESTORED; anything else
 * starts from the Auto positions, so nothing jumps. WHY restore-if-valid
 * rather than always re-seeding: a Custom -> Auto -> Custom round trip is a
 * peek at the Auto layout, and throwing away the heights the author dragged
 * for that would punish looking. WHY not restore a PARTLY valid layout (say,
 * one port added while in Auto): the author never saw it in Custom, so
 * "restore" would invent heights for a layout nobody chose.
 *
 * Not entering (already in Custom): a valid stored layout is used as-is, an
 * invalid one is re-validated (rule 5, `revalidateCustomOffsets`).
 */
export function resolveCustomOffsets(
  stored: Record<string, number>,
  auto: Record<string, number>,
  options: PortSpacingOptions,
  entering: boolean,
): Record<string, number> {
  const validation = { ...options, active: undefined };
  if (customOffsetsValid(stored, validation)) return constrainPortPositions(stored, validation).offsets;
  if (entering) return constrainPortPositions(auto, validation).offsets;
  return revalidateCustomOffsets(stored, auto, validation);
}

/**
 * Where Auto mode's flexbox puts each slot's leading edge, for slots of
 * possibly different sizes — the snapshot Auto -> Custom starts from.
 *
 * `distribute` is `mainAxisPositions` (portEdge.layout.ts, CSS Box Alignment
 * Level 3's content distribution) generalised from one uniform card size to
 * a per-slot size; with uniform sizes the numbers are identical, which
 * portSpacing.layout.test.ts pins. `aligned` packs from its anchor with the
 * explicit gap, after `edgePadding` — exactly what `distributionStyle`
 * emits (the padding on the anchor side moves the content start).
 * `anchored` already IS a leading-edge offset per slot id (`AnchoredConfig`),
 * so its snapshot is those numbers, unchanged.
 */
export function autoSlotOffsets(order: string[], sizes: Record<string, number>, span: number, distribution: Distribution): Record<string, number> {
  if (distribution.mode === "anchored") return Object.fromEntries(order.map((id) => [id, distribution.offsets[id] ?? 0]));
  const n = order.length;
  const size = order.map((id) => sizes[id] ?? 0);
  const total = size.reduce((sum, s) => sum + s, 0);
  let first = 0;
  let gap = 0;
  if (distribution.mode === "aligned") {
    gap = distribution.gap;
    const packed = total + gap * Math.max(n - 1, 0);
    first = distribution.anchor === "start" ? distribution.edgePadding : span - distribution.edgePadding - packed;
  } else if (n > 0) {
    const free = Math.max(span - total, 0);
    if (n === 1) {
      first = distribution.scheme === "space-between" ? 0 : free / 2;
    } else if (distribution.scheme === "space-between") {
      gap = free / (n - 1);
    } else if (distribution.scheme === "space-evenly") {
      gap = free / (n + 1);
      first = gap;
    } else {
      gap = free / n;
      first = gap / 2;
    }
  }
  const offsets: Record<string, number> = {};
  let cursor = first;
  order.forEach((id, i) => {
    offsets[id] = cursor;
    cursor += size[i] + gap;
  });
  return offsets;
}

"use client";

/**
 * src/components/returnDividerMarks.tsx — the PAINT half of "multiple
 * returns"' divider mark: a plain rectangle in the muted-foreground token,
 * "a little grey line" either outside the wall (Expanded) or inward across
 * it (Port view).
 *
 * WHY it lives here and not inline in `blockPort.tsx`: the exact same split
 * `sideEffectMarks.tsx` already uses for its own divider tick
 * (`SideEffectDivider`), and for the reason that file's own header names —
 * `skin.test.ts`'s S4 coverage holds `blockPort.tsx` to "paints nothing of
 * its own — no border, background, box-shadow or stroke". The wrapper
 * PLACES things (`BlockPortReturnDividers`, blockPort.tsx, owns the
 * measured position/size); the paint belongs to a component a skin can be
 * pointed at. FOUND the hard way: this mark originally rendered its
 * `background` directly inside `blockPort.tsx` and broke that exact
 * invariant test (`skin.test.ts`'s "blockPort.tsx paints nothing of its
 * own") — a real, deterministic failure, caught by running the FULL suite
 * rather than only this feature's own stories.
 */
import type { CSSProperties } from "react";

import type { BlockSide } from "./port.layout";

export interface ReturnDividerMarkProps {
  edge: BlockSide;
  markView: "expanded" | "port";
  /** Position and size ONLY — `position`, `left`/`top`, `width`/`height`,
   *  `zIndex`. The paint (`background`) is this component's own; a caller
   *  passing paint here would defeat the whole point of the split. */
  style: CSSProperties;
}

export function ReturnDividerMark({ edge, markView, style }: ReturnDividerMarkProps) {
  return (
    <span
      data-slot="block-port-return-divider"
      data-mark-view={markView}
      data-edge={edge}
      aria-hidden
      style={{ ...style, background: "var(--muted-foreground)", pointerEvents: "none" }}
    />
  );
}

/**
 * src/components/returnDividers.ts — "multiple returns": the pure half of
 * showing a function whose return type is a UNION (`-> joint_angles |
 * None`) as one set of output ports with a DIVIDER mark between the groups.
 *
 * Zach, Daily Note 2026-09-22, "Multiple returns" (`/home/bam/zach_brain/
 * Sources/Daily Notes/Daily Note - Sep 22 2026.md`): "If the return types
 * are the same, then we only need 1 set of output ports. however a | in the
 * output type hint corresponds to a little dash on the outside!" — and, on
 * authoring: "in the multi port line editor if you put a --- or a | that
 * can represent a dividing line."
 *
 * Three unrelated jobs, kept together because they share one vocabulary (a
 * divider is a POSITION between two ports, never a port itself):
 *
 *  1. The RAIL POSITION MODEL (`sanitizeDividers`) — where a divider is
 *     allowed to sit at all.
 *  2. The AUTHORING round trip (`isDividerLine`, `formatRailLane`,
 *     `reconcileRailLane`) — the multi-line lane editor's `---`/`|` line.
 *  3. The UNION-TYPE TRANSLATION (`returnPortGroups`, `railForReturnAnnotation`)
 *     — a declaration string in, port groups out.
 *
 * The RENDERING (the two different drawings, Expanded vs. Port view) is not
 * here — it needs measured DOM geometry (D-A5's "the app may measure
 * itself"), so it lives in `blockPort.tsx` beside its own `GroupSlotBody`/
 * `MutationLines`, which measure for exactly the same reason.
 *
 * (c) ours, ad hoc throughout — no prior art claimed. See
 * `docs/ALGORITHM-PROVENANCE.md`'s own row for this file.
 */
import type { PortEdgeItem } from "./portEdge";
import { LANE_ESCAPE_MARK, encodeLaneLine, portLaneLine, reconcilePortLane } from "./portLane";

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* 1. The rail position model                                          */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

/**
 * A divider "after index k" sits between port `k` and port `k + 1` — so the
 * only INTERIOR positions for `portCount` ports are `0 .. portCount - 2`.
 * "A divider must never end up first or last" is this range, not a special
 * case checked elsewhere: index `-1` (before the first port) and index
 * `portCount - 1` (after the last) are simply not interior, and every
 * caller of this file funnels every divider list through here before it is
 * read, drawn, or round-tripped — so an out-of-range value is dropped once,
 * not re-guarded at each of those sites.
 *
 * Also dedupes and sorts, so `dividerAfter` can be built by an unordered
 * union (as `reconcileRailLane` does) and still compare equal to a hand
 * written literal in a test.
 */
export function sanitizeDividers(dividerAfter: readonly number[], portCount: number): number[] {
  const max = portCount - 2;
  const kept = new Set<number>();
  for (const d of dividerAfter) {
    if (Number.isInteger(d) && d >= 0 && d <= max) kept.add(d);
  }
  return Array.from(kept).sort((a, b) => a - b);
}

/**
 * WHY reordering ports (the Ctrl+arrow chord, `keyboardReorder.ts`) needs no
 * code here at all: `dividerAfter` is POSITIONAL, not attached to any port
 * id. Swapping the ports that occupy slots `i` and `i + 1`
 * (`moveItemOneSlot` in portEdge.tsx, unchanged) never changes which
 * NUMERIC positions are interior, so a divider stays exactly where it was —
 * still separating slot `k` from slot `k + 1` — while the ports on either
 * side of it are free to change. A port "moving across a divider" is
 * therefore just an ordinary move whose neighbour, this time, happens to be
 * on the far side of a fixed boundary: sensible by construction, and the
 * reason `sanitizeDividers` above is the only invariant this feature adds
 * to reordering — it only has teeth again when the PORT COUNT changes
 * (add/remove), never on a pure swap.
 */

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* 2. Authoring — the multi-line lane's `---` / `|` line                */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

const DIVIDER_TOKENS = new Set(["---", "|"]);
/** What a divider line reads back as, whichever of the two tokens typed it
 *  — same idea as `portEdge.layout.ts`'s `groupSyntax` accepting `(a,b)`,
 *  `{a,b}` and `a,b` but never remembering which bracket was typed: the
 *  MODEL has one shape, authoring merely has two spellings for it. */
const DIVIDER_CANONICAL_LINE = "---";

/** A line that means "insert a divider here", typed on its own line. */
export function isDividerLine(line: string): boolean {
  return DIVIDER_TOKENS.has(line.trim());
}

/**
 * A port line that would READ as a divider line, shielded so it does not.
 *
 * `portLane.ts`'s codec escapes the CHARACTER the transport reserves (a real
 * `\n`). The rail reserves a second thing one level up: a whole LINE reading
 * `---` or `|` means "divider here". So a port whose own line reads that way
 * is in-band too — and unshielded it is not merely renamed, it is DELETED:
 * `reconcileRailLane` counts it as a divider, hands `reconcilePortLane` one
 * line fewer, and leaves a boundary standing where the port used to be. Same
 * class as judge finding 1, one layer up, and found by the very sweep that
 * finding's fix required (`returnDividers.test.ts`).
 *
 * The pair is two plain string functions, inverse BY THEIR OWN RULE and
 * independent of the codec beneath them:
 *
 *   shield(line)   = MARK + line, when the line reads as a divider OR
 *                    already begins with MARK; otherwise line, unchanged
 *   unshield(line) = line without ONE leading MARK, if it has one
 *
 * The second half of that condition is what makes them exact inverses rather
 * than nearly so: an encoded label can legitimately BEGIN with the mark
 * (`encodeLaneLine` spells a literal mark `␛␛`), and an unconditional
 * unshield would eat it. Adding a mark whenever one is already there is what
 * keeps "strip exactly one" exactly right.
 *
 * It runs ABOVE the codec on the way out and BELOW it on the way back
 * (format: encode -> shield; reconcile: unshield -> decode), so neither layer
 * has to reason about the other — which is why sharing the `␛` glyph with the
 * codec is a display choice here, not a dependency.
 *
 * Zach's authoring gesture is untouched: "if you put a --- or a | that can
 * represent a dividing line". A line the HUMAN types carries no shield, so it
 * still reads as a divider. Only a line this file EMITTED for a port is ever
 * shielded, and `unshieldPortLine` consumes a raw mark a human typed the same
 * way the codec's decode does.
 */
function shieldPortLine(line: string): string {
  return isDividerLine(line) || line.startsWith(LANE_ESCAPE_MARK) ? LANE_ESCAPE_MARK + line : line;
}

function unshieldPortLine(line: string): string {
  return line.startsWith(LANE_ESCAPE_MARK) ? line.slice(LANE_ESCAPE_MARK.length) : line;
}

/**
 * The lane's document with dividers interleaved — `formatPortLane`
 * (`./portLane.ts`) plus one `---` line per sanitized position. With no
 * dividers this is byte-identical to `formatPortLane(ports)`, which is what
 * keeps every caller that never wires divider support untouched.
 *
 * A port line goes through BOTH escaping layers on the way out —
 * `encodeLaneLine` for the reserved CHARACTER, then `shieldPortLine` for the
 * reserved LINE — and `reconcileRailLane` unwinds them in the mirror order.
 *
 * FIXED, twice, both judge finding 1 (2026-09-22). First: this used to push
 * `portLaneLine(port)` straight into the line list — the RAW label, never run
 * through `encodeLaneLine` — so a port whose label contained a literal `\n`
 * joined the lane text as two physical lines and the next reconcile read it
 * as two different ports, renaming the original and minting a stranger
 * beside it. `encodeLaneLine` is the exact function `formatPortLane` itself
 * uses, shared rather than re-implemented, so the two format functions can
 * never drift apart on what "one line per port" means. Second: a port whose
 * line READ as a divider (`---`, `|`) was worse than renamed — reconcile
 * counted it as a boundary and the port vanished. `shieldPortLine` closes
 * that; see its own doc for why the pair is inverse rather than nearly so.
 *
 * "Byte-identical to `formatPortLane(ports)` with no dividers" therefore
 * holds for every label that is not itself a divider spelling — i.e. every
 * real port name — rather than for all of them.
 */
export function formatRailLane(ports: PortEdgeItem[], dividerAfter: readonly number[]): string {
  const dividers = new Set(sanitizeDividers(dividerAfter, ports.length));
  const lines: string[] = [];
  ports.forEach((port, index) => {
    lines.push(shieldPortLine(encodeLaneLine(portLaneLine(port))));
    if (dividers.has(index)) lines.push(DIVIDER_CANONICAL_LINE);
  });
  return lines.join("\n");
}

export interface RailLaneResult {
  ports: PortEdgeItem[];
  dividerAfter: number[];
}

/**
 * The inverse of `formatRailLane`, built by DELEGATING the port-identity
 * question to the already-tested `reconcilePortLane` (`./portLane.ts`)
 * rather than re-deriving its LCS matching over a mixed line list. Divider
 * lines carry no content to diff against — every one of them reads the
 * literal string `---` (or `|`), so an LCS over the RAW text would match
 * divider lines to each other by coincidence, not by identity. Instead:
 *
 *  1. Strip every divider line out of `text`, remembering, for each
 *     divider, how many PORT lines had been seen so far (`gap`) — the
 *     position "before the `gap`-th surviving port line" (`gap === 0` means
 *     before the very first).
 *  2. Reconcile the PORT-ONLY text exactly as `reconcilePortLane` already
 *     does — new order, renamed lines, minted ports, all untouched.
 *  3. Turn each surviving `gap` into a `dividerAfter` index (`gap - 1`) and
 *     sanitize: `gap === 0` (before the first) and `gap === portCount`
 *     (after the last) both fail `sanitizeDividers`' interior test, which
 *     is "a divider must never end up first or last" happening for free.
 *
 * Consecutive divider lines collapse to the same `gap` (a `Set`) — there is
 * nothing between two touching dividers for a second one to separate.
 */
export function reconcileRailLane(
  ports: PortEdgeItem[],
  dividerAfter: readonly number[],
  text: string,
  mintItem: (label: string) => PortEdgeItem,
): RailLaneResult {
  const rawLines = text === "" ? [] : text.split("\n");
  const portLines: string[] = [];
  const dividerGaps = new Set<number>();
  for (const line of rawLines) {
    // The divider test runs on the RAW line — a shielded port line can never
    // pass it — and only the survivors are unshielded, which puts them back
    // in exactly the spelling `reconcilePortLane` builds its own `before`
    // list in. That parity is what keeps tier-1 LCS matching a shielded port
    // to itself instead of leaving it to tier 2's rank-zip, which mispairs as
    // soon as another line is inserted above it.
    if (isDividerLine(line)) dividerGaps.add(portLines.length);
    else portLines.push(unshieldPortLine(line));
  }

  const nextPorts = reconcilePortLane(ports, portLines.join("\n"), mintItem);
  const interior = Array.from(dividerGaps)
    .filter((gap) => gap > 0 && gap < nextPorts.length)
    .map((gap) => gap - 1);

  return { ports: nextPorts, dividerAfter: sanitizeDividers(interior, nextPorts.length) };
}

/** Order-independent equality for two sanitized divider lists — used where
 *  a caller wants to skip firing a change callback on a genuine no-op. */
export function sameDividers(a: readonly number[], b: readonly number[]): boolean {
  return a.length === b.length && a.every((value, index) => value === b[index]);
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* 3. The union-type translation                                       */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

/**
 * Pulls the return annotation out of a Python-style `def f(...) -> T:` (or
 * `-> T` with no trailing colon). A string with no `->` at all is returned
 * trimmed and unchanged, so a caller may pass either a full signature
 * (`run_ik`'s own sketch) or a bare annotation (`"joint_angles | None"`)
 * through the same function.
 */
export function extractReturnAnnotation(source: string): string {
  const arrow = source.indexOf("->");
  if (arrow === -1) return source.trim();
  const rest = source.slice(arrow + 2);
  const colon = rest.lastIndexOf(":");
  return (colon === -1 ? rest : rest.slice(0, colon)).trim();
}

/** Split on `|` at bracket depth 0 only — PEP 604 union syntax, but a
 *  nested union inside `[...]`/`(...)`/`{...}` (e.g. `dict[str, int |
 *  float] | None`) must not be cut at its own inner `|`. */
function splitTopLevelUnion(text: string): string[] {
  const parts: string[] = [];
  let depth = 0;
  let start = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < text.length; i++) {
    const ch = text[i];
    if (ch === "[" || ch === "(" || ch === "{") depth++;
    else if (ch === "]" || ch === ")" || ch === "}") depth = Math.max(0, depth - 1);
    else if (ch === "|" && depth === 0) {
      parts.push(text.slice(start, i));
      start = i + 1;
    }
  }
  parts.push(text.slice(start));
  return parts;
}

export interface ReturnPort {
  id: string;
  label: string;
}

/**
 * A declaration string in, port GROUPS out — one group per DISTINCT union
 * member (by `normalizedTypeKey`, not raw text), in first-appearance order.
 * "If the return types are the same, then we only need 1 set of output
 * ports" (Zach, above) is read as a statement about the whole union, not
 * just neighbours and not just identical bytes: `bool | int | bool` folds
 * its repeated `bool` into the group it already has rather than minting a
 * second `bool` port, `list[ int ] | list[int]` folds the same way despite
 * the spacing difference, so `run_ik`'s literal case (`joint_angles |
 * joint_angles`, hypothetically) collapses to ONE group — and, downstream
 * in `railForReturnAnnotation`, no divider — for the same reason a
 * single-member annotation (no `|` at all) always has.
 *
 * Every group here is a singleton today (`splitTopLevelUnion` never groups
 * more than one member under one name) — plural "groups" is still the right
 * shape, not a premature one: it is the same shape `railForReturnAnnotation`
 * needs regardless of whether a future structured return (a NamedTuple's
 * several fields, say) ever makes a group hold more than one port.
 */
/**
 * The DEDUPE key for a union member — INSIGNIFICANT whitespace collapsed
 * away, so `list[ int ]` and `list[int]` read as the SAME type. `label`
 * itself is never rewritten to this form; the whole point (below) is that
 * the FIRST authored spelling is what stays on screen.
 *
 * FIXED, twice. `returnPortGroups` originally deduped on the trimmed label
 * BYTE FOR BYTE, so `list[ int ] | list[int]` — the same type, spaced
 * differently — produced two ports and a divider between them (judge finding
 * 2, first half). The fix for that deleted EVERY whitespace character,
 * `/\s+/g` across the whole label, which over-collapses in the other
 * direction (judge finding 2, second half, 2026-09-22):
 * `Literal["a b"] | Literal["ab"]` are two DIFFERENT types and got one key,
 * so the rail drew one port and no divider, and a space the author owns went
 * missing from the identity test.
 *
 * Whitespace is insignificant BETWEEN the tokens of a type expression and
 * significant INSIDE a string literal. That is the line this scanner draws,
 * and it is the whole rule.
 *
 * Ours, ad hoc — nothing ported, no implementation read, and none claimed
 * (see this file's header). The rule it encodes is Python's own lexical one:
 * whitespace separates tokens and is otherwise ignored, while a string
 * literal's contents are DATA, every character of them part of the value.
 * That is checkable in any Python REPL — `Literal["a b"]` and `Literal["ab"]`
 * are different types — rather than a citation of a parser we looked at.
 *
 * Two things it deliberately does NOT normalize, because either needs real
 * literal EVALUATION rather than a scan:
 *  - QUOTE STYLE. `Literal['a b']` and `Literal["a b"]` are one type to
 *    Python and get two keys here. Folding them means deciding what `"a\"b"`
 *    and `'a"b'` have in common, which is a string evaluator, not a scanner.
 *  - An UNTERMINATED quote (`Literal["a b`, a half-typed annotation). Every
 *    character after the opening quote is kept verbatim: when the text cannot
 *    be parsed, preserving the author's bytes is the conservative answer, and
 *    two half-typed spellings simply stay distinct.
 * Both are pinned by tests so they read as decisions, not as oversights.
 */
const INSIGNIFICANT_WHITESPACE = /\s/;
const QUOTE_CHARACTERS = new Set(['"', "'"]);

function normalizedTypeKey(label: string): string {
  let key = "";
  let openQuote: string | null = null;
  for (let index = 0; index < label.length; index++) {
    const character = label[index];
    if (openQuote !== null) {
      key += character;
      // A backslash takes the next character with it, so `\"` cannot close
      // the literal it is escaping — without this, the rest of a quoted
      // string would be treated as code and its spaces collapsed.
      if (character === "\\" && index + 1 < label.length) {
        key += label[index + 1];
        index++;
      } else if (character === openQuote) {
        openQuote = null;
      }
      continue;
    }
    if (QUOTE_CHARACTERS.has(character)) {
      openQuote = character;
      key += character;
      continue;
    }
    if (INSIGNIFICANT_WHITESPACE.test(character)) continue;
    key += character;
  }
  return key;
}

export function returnPortGroups(annotation: string): ReturnPort[][] {
  const text = annotation.trim();
  if (text === "") return [];
  const groups: ReturnPort[][] = [];
  const seen = new Set<string>();
  for (const raw of splitTopLevelUnion(text)) {
    const label = raw.trim();
    if (label === "") continue;
    const key = normalizedTypeKey(label);
    if (seen.has(key)) continue;
    seen.add(key);
    // `id`/`label` keep the FIRST authored spelling — the displayed text is
    // never the normalized key, only the test FOR sameness is.
    groups.push([{ id: label, label }]);
  }
  return groups;
}

export interface ReturnRail {
  items: PortEdgeItem[];
  dividerAfter: number[];
}

/**
 * Flattens `returnPortGroups`' output into one ordered `items` list plus
 * the `dividerAfter` positions BETWEEN groups — "the rail shows one set of
 * output ports with a divider between the groups." A single-group
 * annotation (no `|`, or a union that folded to one distinct member) comes
 * out with an EMPTY `dividerAfter` for free: "no divider" is a consequence
 * of there being one group, not a second rule this function has to enforce.
 */
export function railForReturnAnnotation(source: string, state?: PortEdgeItem["state"]): ReturnRail {
  const groups = returnPortGroups(extractReturnAnnotation(source));
  const items: PortEdgeItem[] = [];
  const dividerAfter: number[] = [];
  groups.forEach((group, groupIndex) => {
    for (const port of group) items.push({ id: port.id, label: port.label, ...(state ? { state } : null) });
    if (groupIndex < groups.length - 1) dividerAfter.push(items.length - 1);
  });
  return { items, dividerAfter: sanitizeDividers(dividerAfter, items.length) };
}

"use client";

/**
 * src/components/sideEffectMarks.tsx — the two marks side effects add to a
 * Block that are PAINT, not ports: the divider tick on the top wall and the
 * dotted mutation line. `BlockPort` decides where they go
 * (`sideEffects.layout.ts`) and mounts them; this file decides what they
 * look like.
 *
 * WHY they live here and not inline in `blockPort.tsx`: `skin.test.ts`'s S4
 * coverage holds `blockPort.tsx` to "paints nothing of its own — no border,
 * background, box-shadow or stroke". The wrapper places things; the paint
 * belongs to a component a skin can be pointed at. Both marks read theme
 * tokens only (`--muted-foreground`, `--destructive`), so dark mode needs
 * nothing extra.
 */
import { SIDE_EFFECT_DIVIDER_PX, type MutationRoute } from "./sideEffects.layout";

/** The short grey tick splitting input side effects from output ones —
 *  "a little grey line on the outside" (Zach, 2026-09-22). Its foot sits on
 *  the wall line at `x`; the tick sticks out above it. */
export function SideEffectDivider({ x }: { x: number }) {
  return (
    <span
      data-slot="block-side-effect-divider"
      aria-hidden
      style={{
        position: "absolute",
        left: x - 1,
        top: -SIDE_EFFECT_DIVIDER_PX,
        width: 2,
        height: SIDE_EFFECT_DIVIDER_PX,
        background: "var(--muted-foreground)",
        pointerEvents: "none",
      }}
    />
  );
}

/** One mutation line: dotted, in the destructive token, with a filled
 *  arrowhead at the effect end. Geometry arrives resolved. */
export function MutationLineMark({ from, to, route }: { from: string; to: string; route: MutationRoute }) {
  return (
    <g data-slot="block-mutation-line" data-mutation-from={from} data-mutation-to={to}>
      <polyline
        points={route.points.map((p) => `${p.x},${p.y}`).join(" ")}
        fill="none"
        stroke="var(--destructive)"
        strokeWidth={1.5}
        strokeDasharray="2 3"
        strokeLinecap="round"
      />
      <polygon points={route.arrowhead.map((p) => `${p.x},${p.y}`).join(" ")} fill="var(--destructive)" />
    </g>
  );
}

/**
 * src/components/sideEffects.layout.ts — the pure half of a Block's
 * SIDE-EFFECT ports: the model (what a side effect and a mutation link ARE)
 * and the two pieces of geometry `BlockPort` draws them with. No React, no
 * DOM, no canvas engine — every function here runs in plain Node.
 *
 * Zach, 2026-09-22: "add first class support for output and input side
 * effect ports." And for where they go: "Draw a little grey line on the
 * outside to split between input and output side effects. Splits down the
 * middle. If no inputs then just assume its output…. Well it's fine honestly
 * you can put it towards the back end… always its ok…"
 *
 * WHAT WAS HERE BEFORE: nothing. `elbow/boundaryCrossing.ts:25` names "a
 * side-effect port, which appears where an effect cable leaves its block" as
 * one future CALLER of its crossing maths — a DERIVED port that moves when
 * you drag the cable. This file is the other reading, the one Zach sketched
 * on 2026-09-22: a DECLARED port with a stable id and a fixed seat on the top
 * wall. The two do not conflict; nothing here imports or replaces that file.
 *
 * PROVENANCE (docs/ALGORITHM-PROVENANCE.md, row `sideEffects.layout.ts`):
 *  - `sideEffectWallLayout` is class (b): each half of the wall is CSS Box
 *    Alignment's `space-evenly`, computed by calling
 *    `portEdge.layout.ts`'s `mainAxisPositions` — the kit's existing,
 *    already-cited implementation (§1 of that doc) — not a second copy of
 *    it. The split at the midpoint is Zach's own sentence above.
 *  - `mutationRoute` is class (c), ours, ad hoc: a one-bend orthogonal L,
 *    horizontal first and then vertical, which is the route Zach drew. No
 *    prior art is claimed for a two-segment polyline.
 */
import { mainAxisPositions } from "./portEdge.layout";
import type { PortState } from "./port";

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* The model                                                           */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

/**
 * Which way a side effect flows: state the block READS from outside its
 * dataflow (`input`), or state it WRITES to outside it (`output`).
 *
 * WHY its own two words and not `scene/model.ts`'s `PortDirection`
 * (`source | sink`): that vocabulary is a wire's orientation at a port, and
 * a side effect's wire runs the OTHER way round from its name — an input
 * side effect is a wire's SINK, an output side effect its SOURCE. Reusing
 * `source`/`sink` here would make every reader do that inversion in their
 * head. Zach's own words are "input and output side effects", so those are
 * the words.
 */
export type SideEffectDirection = "input" | "output";

/** One side-effect port. `id` is the identity — stamped as `data-port-id`,
 *  so a wire names it exactly as it names any rail port. */
export interface SideEffectPort {
  id: string;
  /** Painted above the wall beside the dot, character for character. Omit
   *  for a bare dot. */
  label?: string;
  /** The same four-value axis every rail port uses. `wired` fills the dot;
   *  omitted reads `empty` (hollow). */
  state?: PortState;
}

/**
 * A block's side effects, keyed by direction.
 *
 * WHY keyed and not a flat `{ id, direction }[]`: it is the exact shape
 * `BlockPortProps.edges` already uses for ordinary ports — `edges.left`,
 * `edges.right` — where the KEY is the placement fact and each list is in
 * authored order. Keyed, an effect cannot be listed as an input while also
 * claiming to be an output, and "every input sits left of the divider"
 * is one list, not a filter a renderer might get wrong.
 */
export interface BlockSideEffects {
  inputs?: readonly SideEffectPort[];
  outputs?: readonly SideEffectPort[];
}

/**
 * "This input is mutated, and leaves through that output side effect." A
 * typed relation between two ids on ONE block: `from` is an ordinary input
 * port (an item on the block's left wall), `to` an OUTPUT side effect.
 *
 * WHY a sibling list on the block and not a field on the input port: the
 * relation is many-to-many in principle (one input mutated into two
 * effects, two inputs into one), and a field on either end could hold only
 * one of those. It is the same reason a `Wire` lives beside its blocks
 * rather than on a port.
 */
export interface MutationLink {
  from: string;
  to: string;
}

/** The direction an effect id is declared with, or `undefined` if this
 *  block declares no such effect. */
export function sideEffectDirectionOf(effects: BlockSideEffects | undefined, id: string): SideEffectDirection | undefined {
  if (effects?.inputs?.some((effect) => effect.id === id)) return "input";
  if (effects?.outputs?.some((effect) => effect.id === id)) return "output";
  return undefined;
}

/** True when the block declares at least one side effect of either kind —
 *  the condition the divider tick is drawn on. */
export function hasSideEffects(effects: BlockSideEffects | undefined): boolean {
  return (effects?.inputs?.length ?? 0) + (effects?.outputs?.length ?? 0) > 0;
}

/**
 * Split `links` into the ones this block can draw and the ones it cannot.
 * A link resolves when `from` is one of `inputPortIds` and `to` is an
 * OUTPUT side effect of this block. Anything else — a typo, a link aimed at
 * an INPUT effect, a port that is not on the input wall — is returned in
 * `unresolved` and drawn nowhere, never guessed at (the same rule
 * `wireLayer.tsx` applies to a wire end it cannot find).
 */
export function partitionMutations(
  links: readonly MutationLink[] | undefined,
  effects: BlockSideEffects | undefined,
  inputPortIds: readonly string[],
): { resolved: MutationLink[]; unresolved: MutationLink[] } {
  const resolved: MutationLink[] = [];
  const unresolved: MutationLink[] = [];
  for (const link of links ?? []) {
    const ok = inputPortIds.includes(link.from) && sideEffectDirectionOf(effects, link.to) === "output";
    (ok ? resolved : unresolved).push(link);
  }
  return { resolved, unresolved };
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* The top wall — where every dot and the divider sit                   */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

/** How far the divider tick sticks out ABOVE the top wall, px. */
export const SIDE_EFFECT_DIVIDER_PX = 10;

export interface SideEffectWallLayout {
  /** The divider tick's x — the wall's own horizontal centre. */
  dividerX: number;
  /** Dot-centre x of each input, in authored order. All `< dividerX`. */
  inputXs: number[];
  /** Dot-centre x of each output, in authored order. All `> dividerX`. */
  outputXs: number[];
}

/**
 * Where each side-effect dot centres along the top wall, in WALL-LOCAL px
 * (0 = the block's left border line). The wall splits down the middle:
 * inputs share the left half and outputs the right half, each half
 * `space-evenly`, so a lone input lands at the quarter point and a lone
 * output at the three-quarter point — with outputs only, they still sit
 * right of the centre, "towards the back end".
 *
 * WHY each half is spread rather than packed at a fixed pitch: a label sits
 * beside its dot, growing away from the divider (`blockPort.tsx`'s
 * `SideEffectDot`), so the room a label has is the distance to the next dot
 * out. Spreading over the half hands every label the most room the wall
 * has; a fixed pitch would crowd a short wall and strand a long one.
 */
export function sideEffectWallLayout(wallWidth: number, inputCount: number, outputCount: number): SideEffectWallLayout {
  const half = Math.max(wallWidth, 0) / 2;
  return {
    dividerX: half,
    inputXs: mainAxisPositions(inputCount, half, 0, "space-evenly"),
    outputXs: mainAxisPositions(outputCount, half, 0, "space-evenly").map((x) => half + x),
  };
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* The mutation line — one bend, horizontal then vertical               */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

export interface MutationPoint {
  x: number;
  y: number;
}

/** The arrowhead's length along the line and its full width, px. */
export const MUTATION_ARROW_LENGTH_PX = 6;
export const MUTATION_ARROW_WIDTH_PX = 6;

export interface MutationRoute {
  /** The polyline: start, bend, end. */
  points: [MutationPoint, MutationPoint, MutationPoint];
  /** The arrowhead triangle, tip first; the tip IS `points[2]`. */
  arrowhead: [MutationPoint, MutationPoint, MutationPoint];
}

/**
 * The mutation line from an input port's dot to an output side effect's
 * dot: horizontal from the port, then vertical to the effect — the route
 * Zach drew ("runs from the 3rd LEFT-WALL port horizontally through the
 * body, then up to `out 1`").
 *
 * `fromRadiusPx`/`toRadiusPx` pull each end back to its dot's EDGE on the
 * side the line approaches from, so the line starts at the input dot's rim
 * and the arrowhead's tip touches the effect dot's rim rather than
 * disappearing under the dot that paints over it.
 */
export function mutationRoute(from: MutationPoint, to: MutationPoint, fromRadiusPx: number, toRadiusPx: number): MutationRoute {
  const towardX = Math.sign(to.x - from.x) || 1;
  const towardY = Math.sign(to.y - from.y) || -1;
  const start = { x: from.x + towardX * fromRadiusPx, y: from.y };
  const bend = { x: to.x, y: from.y };
  const tip = { x: to.x, y: to.y - towardY * toRadiusPx };
  const base = tip.y - towardY * MUTATION_ARROW_LENGTH_PX;
  const halfWidth = MUTATION_ARROW_WIDTH_PX / 2;
  return {
    points: [start, bend, tip],
    arrowhead: [tip, { x: tip.x - halfWidth, y: base }, { x: tip.x + halfWidth, y: base }],
  };
}

// src/components/tooltip.tsx — copied verbatim from
// apps/docs/src/registry/new-york-v4/ui/tooltip.tsx (the stock shadcn
// `new-york` Tooltip over Base UI's Tooltip primitive). packages/inspector
// also has a tooltip.tsx, but it's coupled to the dock's own portal
// container and z-index stack (`dockPortalContainer()`, `z-[320]`) — plumbing
// this standalone app doesn't have, so the docs version is the cleaner,
// self-contained pick. Only the `cn` import path changed to match this
// app's own src/lib/utils.ts.
"use client";

import { Tooltip as TooltipPrimitive } from "@base-ui/react/tooltip";

import { cn } from "../lib/utils";

function TooltipProvider({ delay = 0, ...props }: TooltipPrimitive.Provider.Props) {
  return <TooltipPrimitive.Provider data-slot="tooltip-provider" delay={delay} {...props} />;
}

function Tooltip({ ...props }: TooltipPrimitive.Root.Props) {
  return <TooltipPrimitive.Root data-slot="tooltip" {...props} />;
}

function TooltipTrigger({ ...props }: TooltipPrimitive.Trigger.Props) {
  return <TooltipPrimitive.Trigger data-slot="tooltip-trigger" {...props} />;
}

function TooltipContent({
  className,
  side = "top",
  sideOffset = 4,
  align = "center",
  alignOffset = 0,
  anchor,
  collisionPadding,
  container,
  children,
  style,
  ...props
}: TooltipPrimitive.Popup.Props &
  // WHY `anchor` was added to this Pick (2026-09-18, menu kit build): same
  // reason as dropdown-menu.tsx's matching edit — a canvas-anchored hint (a
  // pointer-position tooltip with no DOM trigger) needs Positioner's own
  // `anchor` (a Floating UI VirtualElement), which this Pick never
  // surfaced. Adds-only; every current consumer that omits `anchor` is
  // unaffected.
  //
  // `collisionPadding` and `container` followed in the same pass that fixed
  // dropdown-menu.tsx — see the longer note there. The pair has to stay in
  // lockstep across these two files: a host that scopes its design tokens
  // to a container element and gets themed MENUS but unthemed TOOLTIPS has
  // a bug that is much harder to read than one where neither works.
  Pick<
    TooltipPrimitive.Positioner.Props,
    "align" | "alignOffset" | "side" | "sideOffset" | "anchor" | "collisionPadding"
  > &
  Pick<TooltipPrimitive.Portal.Props, "container">) {
  return (
    <TooltipPrimitive.Portal container={container}>
      <TooltipPrimitive.Positioner
        align={align}
        alignOffset={alignOffset}
        side={side}
        sideOffset={sideOffset}
        anchor={anchor}
        collisionPadding={collisionPadding}
        className="isolate z-50"
      >
        <TooltipPrimitive.Popup
          data-slot="tooltip-content"
          className={cn(
            "data-open:animate-in data-open:fade-in-0 data-open:zoom-in-95 data-[state=delayed-open]:animate-in data-[state=delayed-open]:fade-in-0 data-[state=delayed-open]:zoom-in-95 data-closed:animate-out data-closed:fade-out-0 data-closed:zoom-out-95 data-[side=bottom]:slide-in-from-top-2 data-[side=left]:slide-in-from-right-2 data-[side=right]:slide-in-from-left-2 data-[side=top]:slide-in-from-bottom-2 inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 text-xs has-data-[slot=kbd]:pr-1.5 **:data-[slot=kbd]:relative **:data-[slot=kbd]:isolate **:data-[slot=kbd]:z-50 **:data-[slot=kbd]:rounded-sm data-[side=inline-start]:slide-in-from-right-2 data-[side=inline-end]:slide-in-from-left-2 z-50 w-fit max-w-xs origin-(--transform-origin) bg-foreground text-background",
            className,
          )}
          // The skin seam (src/skin/skin.ts + skin.css) — `rounded-md`
          // moved off the class list above and onto here so a skin can
          // reach it; `var(--radius-md)` is exactly what it resolved to,
          // so a consumer who never imports skin.css is byte-identical.
          style={{ borderRadius: "var(--bbox-skin-radius, var(--radius-md))", ...style }}
          {...props}
        >
          {children}
          <TooltipPrimitive.Arrow className="size-2.5 translate-y-[calc(-50%-2px)] rotate-45 rounded-[2px] data-[side=inline-end]:top-1/2! data-[side=inline-end]:-left-1 data-[side=inline-end]:-translate-y-1/2 data-[side=inline-start]:top-1/2! data-[side=inline-start]:-right-1 data-[side=inline-start]:-translate-y-1/2 z-50 bg-foreground fill-foreground data-[side=bottom]:top-1 data-[side=left]:top-1/2! data-[side=left]:-right-1 data-[side=left]:-translate-y-1/2 data-[side=right]:top-1/2! data-[side=right]:-left-1 data-[side=right]:-translate-y-1/2 data-[side=top]:-bottom-2.5" />
        </TooltipPrimitive.Popup>
      </TooltipPrimitive.Positioner>
    </TooltipPrimitive.Portal>
  );
}

export { Tooltip, TooltipContent, TooltipProvider, TooltipTrigger };

import { clsx, type ClassValue } from "clsx";
import { twMerge } from "tailwind-merge";

export function cn(...inputs: ClassValue[]) {
  return twMerge(clsx(inputs));
}

// src/skin/portVariants.ts — the port's VISUAL VARIANT registry: five
// circular, on-the-edge port styles, selectable by one attribute on
// `<html>` (`<html data-bbox-port-variant="v3">`) or by an explicit
// `variant` prop, defaulting to `v1`.
//
// Zach, 2026-09-19 (docs/ASK-2026-09-19-PORTS-EDGES-REGIONS-FEEDBACK.md):
// "the ports look way too big right now and the port text size also seems
// too big … generally it looks very unprofessional … I want the port text
// to be 'small' by default … one proposal should be based on the original
// SystemSketch source code, one on langflow … I only want to see proposals
// that explore circular ports … proposals where the ports are on the edge,
// not where they are inset."
//
// WHAT A VARIANT DECIDES — and only this: the dot's box diameter, the two
// rings painted OUTSIDE that box (the card-coloured `gap`, then the `ring`),
// which PAINT GRAMMAR maps the value state (empty · valueSet · wired ·
// received) onto those three circles, the label/type typography, and the
// hover / selected / producers-circle treatments. It never decides WHERE a
// dot sits: every variant is centred ON the block wall, half outside, by
// `BlockPort`'s rail geometry — the one rule that is not a proposal (the
// anti-example is `115744`, square handles inset inside the node body).
//
// WHY every variant keeps the SAME text rung (`xs`, 12px): "small by
// default" is the one thing Zach ruled outright, and the next rung up
// (`sm`, 18px) is exactly the size he called too big. So the five vary
// what a reviewer actually compares at that size — dot scale (11 → 16px
// painted), fill grammar, colour source (state vs. type), typeface and
// weight — and the text never jumps when he flips between them.
//
// WHY CSS custom properties AND a typed table, not one or the other: the
// components need NUMBERS (a label is placed from the dot's box, the sketch
// skin's boundary pass reads `data-bbox-ring-expand`), so this table is the
// source of truth and every port root publishes its resolved variant as
// inline `--bbox-port-*` properties. `portVariants.css` carries the SAME
// values under `[data-bbox-port-variant]` blocks for CSS-only consumers
// (the regions lane's frame inset, a host's chrome) and holds the
// hover/selected/producers rules that read them. `PortVariants.stories.tsx`'s
// `CssMirrorAgrees` pins the two together, the way `skin.test.ts` pins
// skin.css to skin.ts.
//
// WHY a MutationObserver-backed hook rather than React context: the ASK's
// contract is "CSS custom properties + data attributes, no React context
// needed — so the app switch and the Storybook global are three lines
// each". A host stamps the attribute; every mounted port re-resolves on its
// own. `useSyncExternalStore` makes that a real subscription, not a
// read-once-at-mount.
//
// PROVENANCE — every number below was read from a file on disk or a source
// fetched on 2026-09-19 (paths are the ones this lane read; the langflow
// clone is a shallow `git clone` of langflow-ai/langflow into the session
// scratchpad, cited by its repo-relative path):
//
//   v1  React Flow's own handle SCALE — `@xyflow/react/dist/style.css:250-251`
//       (`width: 6px; height: 6px`), `:274-278` (`.react-flow__handle-left
//       { left: 0; transform: translate(-50%, -50%) }` — centred ON the
//       edge) — drawn in THIS trunk's three-circle state grammar
//       (`port.tsx` STATE_TOKENS; `DP - Port.md` "Anatomy": outline,
//       background, centre) so every value state stays readable and the
//       colour still comes from STATE, per the 2026-09-10 ruling recorded
//       in `systemsketch/src/bboxUi/core/port.tsx:58-64`.
//   v2  the original SystemSketch live port —
//       `systemsketch/src/blocks/ui/block-canvas.css:1099-1132` ("The 12px
//       pyblocks dot: hollow while unwired, neutral-filled when it owns a
//       default, type-filled once a cable lands. The surface gap and
//       type-coloured outer ring…": 12px disc, `0 0 0 2px surface`, `0 0 0
//       3px var(--port-color)`), its palette `systemsketch/src/blocks/ui/
//       portPalette.ts:23-40`, the monospace signature row
//       `block-canvas.css:1035-1052` (`.BlockNode-portName` /
//       `.BlockNode-portType`, ui-monospace, type muted), and the count pill
//       `block-canvas.css:1453-1478` (`.Port-count`: surface-active fill,
//       muted 600 text, riding OUTSIDE the dot). Scaled ×2/3 with the text
//       (18px → 12px): disc 12 → 8, gap 2 → 1, ring 1 → 1.
//   v3  langflow — `src/frontend/src/CustomNodes/GenericNode/components/
//       handleRenderComponent/index.tsx`: `:128-131` a 10px `rounded-full`
//       disc whose background IS the type colour, `:110-113` a resting
//       `0 0 0 3px <datatype-X-foreground>` pale ring, `:114-123` a neon
//       glow only while hovered/open, `:24-32` a 32px transparent hit target
//       (our 40px halo); the colour names `src/frontend/src/utils/
//       styleUtils.ts:171-215` (`nodeColorsName`) → `src/frontend/src/style/
//       index.css:162-202` (`--datatype-*` light HSL pairs); unknown types
